    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nObject cloning is the process of creating an exact copy of an existing object's state in a new object, without invoking a constructor. Java supports this through the Cloneable marker interface and the clone() method defined in the Object class. By default, calling clone() performs a shallow copy \u2014 primitive fields are copied by value, but reference fields (objects, arrays) are copied by reference, so both the original and the clone point to the same nested objects. To achieve a deep copy, you must override clone() and explicitly clone the nested mutable objects as well.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nclass Employee implements Cloneable {\n    String name;\n    Address address;\n    // mutable reference type\n    public Object clone() throws CloneNotSupportedException {\n        return super.clone();\n        // shallow copy - address is shared\n    }\n}\nFor a deep copy, inside clone() you'd write: Employee copy = (Employee) super.clone(); copy.address = new Address(this.address); return copy;\n```",
