    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThe Stream API, introduced in Java 8, provides a functional-style way to process a sequence of elements with operations such as filter, map, sorted, reduce, and collect. A stream is not a collection or data structure; it represents a pipeline for processing data. Intermediate operations such as filter(), map(), and sorted() are generally lazy, while a terminal operation such as collect(), reduce(), or forEach() starts the pipeline. Streams can also be parallel, but parallelStream() should not be assumed to be faster; it is most useful when the workload is large enough and the operations are suitable for parallel execution.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nList<String> names = List.of(\"Alice\", \"Bob\", \"Charlie\", \"Dave\");\nList<String> result = names.stream() .filter(n -> n.length() > 3) .map(String::toUpperCase) .sorted() .collect(Collectors.toList());\n// result = [ALICE, CHARLIE, DAVE]\n```",
