    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nSpring makes extensive use of classic Gang-of-Four design patterns internally and encourages their use in application code. Key patterns include: Singleton (Spring beans are singleton-scoped by default, one instance per container), Factory/FactoryBean (BeanFactory and ApplicationContext act as factories that create and manage beans), Proxy (used for AOP \u2014 Spring wraps beans in dynamic proxies to add cross-cutting behavior like transactions or security), Template Method (JdbcTemplate, RestTemplate abstract away boilerplate steps), Dependency Injection / Inversion of Control (the core Spring pattern, where dependencies are provided externally rather than created internally), Observer (ApplicationEvent and ApplicationListener), and Decorator/Adapter for wrapping and adapting components like HandlerAdapter in Spring MVC.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@Service public class OrderService {\n    private final PaymentGateway paymentGateway;\n    // dependency injected, not \"newed\" internally\n    public OrderService(PaymentGateway paymentGateway) {\n        // Dependency Injection pattern this.paymentGateway = paymentGateway;\n    }\n}\n// Spring's IoC container (a Factory pattern) creates and wires OrderService automatically.\n```",
