    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nException is the superclass for all checked exceptions (plus it's also the parent of RuntimeException). Checked exceptions (like IOException, SQLException) must be either caught with a try-catch or declared in the method signature using \"throws\" \u2014 the compiler enforces this at compile time, because they represent recoverable conditions the caller should anticipate (like a missing file). RuntimeException and its subclasses (like NullPointerException, IllegalArgumentException, ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException) are unchecked exceptions \u2014 the compiler does not force you to handle or declare them, because they usually indicate programming bugs rather than recoverable external conditions.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Checked - must declare or\ncatch public void readFile(String path) throws IOException {\n    FileReader fr = new FileReader(path);\n    //\n    throws checked IOException\n}\n// Unchecked - compiler doesn't force handling\npublic int divide(int a, int b) {\n    return a / b;\n    // may\n    throw unchecked ArithmeticException at runtime, no declaration needed\n}\n```",
