    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA common approach is the sliding window or fixed window counter algorithm backed by a fast in-memory store like Redis, since rate limiting must work correctly across multiple application instances in a distributed system. Each client (identified by API key, user ID, or IP) gets a counter key with a 5-minute expiry (TTL). On each request, you atomically increment the counter (Redis INCR is atomic); if the counter exceeds 1000 within that window, you reject the request with HTTP 429 Too Many Requests. A more accurate variant is the sliding-window-log or token-bucket algorithm, which avoids the \"burst at window boundary\" problem of fixed windows. In Spring Boot, this can be implemented with a custom filter/interceptor combined with Redis, or by using a library like Bucket4j or Resilience4j RateLimiter.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nString key = \"rate_limit:\" + clientId;\nLong count = redisTemplate.opsForValue().increment(key);\nif (count == 1) {\n    redisTemplate.expire(key, Duration.ofMinutes(5));\n}\nif (count > 1000) {\n    throw new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS);\n}\n```",
