    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA functional interface is an interface that contains exactly one abstract method, making it eligible to be implemented using a lambda expression or method reference. It may contain any number of default or static methods, but only one abstract method. Java provides the @FunctionalInterface annotation (optional but recommended) which causes the compiler to flag an error if more than one abstract method is accidentally added. The java.util.function package provides many built-in functional interfaces like Function, Predicate, Supplier, and Consumer.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@FunctionalInterface interface Calculator {\n    int operate(int a, int b);\n}\nCalculator addition = (a, b) -> a + b;\n// lambda implementing the single abstract method System.out.println(addition.operate(3, 4));\n// prints 7\n```",
