    explanation: "This question evaluates how you would handle how is hibernate connection-pool sizing determined in a real Java application. A strong answer should make the assumptions explicit, explain the design or troubleshooting sequence, identify failure modes and trade-offs, and finish with how you would validate the result in production.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nConnection-pool sizing is a capacity-planning problem rather than a fixed Java or Hibernate formula. In a Spring Boot application, HikariCP is commonly the default pool when it is available, but the pool is provided by the application stack rather than being a \"Hibernate default.\" Start with a deliberately small pool, then tune it using database connection limits, CPU/I/O capacity, query latency, expected concurrency, and measurements from load tests. HikariCP's commonly cited sizing guidance can be a useful starting point, but it should not be treated as a universal formula. Monitor active, idle, pending, and timed-out connections and make sure the total pool sizes across all application instances stay within the database's safe capacity.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```text\n# HikariCP configuration in application.properties\nspring.datasource.hikari.maximum-pool-size=15\nspring.datasource.hikari.minimum-idle=5\nspring.datasource.hikari.connection-timeout=30000\nspring.datasource.hikari.idle-timeout=600000\nspring.datasource.hikari.max-lifetime=1800000\n// You'd load-test and watch pool metrics (via Actuator) to tune the exact number.\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nConnection-pool sizing is a capacity-planning problem rather than a fixed Java or Hibernate formula. In a Spring Boot application, HikariCP is commonly the default pool when it is available, but the pool is provided by the application stack rather than being a \"Hibernate default.\" Start with a deliberately small pool, then tune it using database connection limits, CPU/I/O capacity, query latency, expected concurrency, and measurements from load tests. HikariCP's commonly cited sizing guidance can be a useful starting point, but it should not be treated as a universal formula. Monitor active, idle, pending, and timed-out connections and make sure the total pool sizes across all application instances stay within the database's safe capacity.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```text\n# HikariCP configuration in application.properties\nspring.datasource.hikari.maximum-pool-size=15\nspring.datasource.hikari.minimum-idle=5\nspring.datasource.hikari.connection-timeout=30000\nspring.datasource.hikari.idle-timeout=600000\nspring.datasource.hikari.max-lifetime=1800000\n// You'd load-test and watch pool metrics (via Actuator) to tune the exact number.\n```",
