    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "A focused Exceptions & Error Handling interview question about what is try-with-resources and why use it, covering the core concept, how it behaves in Java, and the practical distinction an interviewer is likely to probe.",
    explanation: "This question evaluates your understanding of what is try-with-resources and why use it in Java. A strong answer should define the concept precisely, explain how it behaves in practice, distinguish it from commonly confused concepts, and give a concrete example or consequence that shows you understand it rather than memorizing a definition.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\ntry-with-resources is a Java 7+ syntax that automatically closes resources (any object implementing AutoCloseable, such as streams, connections, or readers) at the end of the try block, even if an exception occurs \u2014 eliminating the need for manual finally blocks to close resources. This prevents resource leaks (like unclosed file handles or database connections) that are easy to forget with manual try-finally, and it also correctly handles suppressed exceptions if both the resource's close() and the try block throw exceptions.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Old way - verbose and error-prone\nBufferedReader br = null;\ntry {\n    br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(\"file.txt\"));\n    // read file\n}\nfinally {\n    if (br != null) br.close();\n}\n//\ntry-with-resources - cleaner, automatic close try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(\"file.txt\"))) {\n    System.out.println(br.readLine());\n}\n// br.close() called automatically here, even\nif an exception is thrown\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\ntry-with-resources is a Java 7+ syntax that automatically closes resources (any object implementing AutoCloseable, such as streams, connections, or readers) at the end of the try block, even if an exception occurs \u2014 eliminating the need for manual finally blocks to close resources. This prevents resource leaks (like unclosed file handles or database connections) that are easy to forget with manual try-finally, and it also correctly handles suppressed exceptions if both the resource's close() and the try block throw exceptions.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Old way - verbose and error-prone\nBufferedReader br = null;\ntry {\n    br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(\"file.txt\"));\n    // read file\n}\nfinally {\n    if (br != null) br.close();\n}\n//\ntry-with-resources - cleaner, automatic close try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(\"file.txt\"))) {\n    System.out.println(br.readLine());\n}\n// br.close() called automatically here, even\nif an exception is thrown\n```",
