    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThese describe the two fundamental ways classes relate to each other in OOP. IS-A represents inheritance \u2014 a subclass \"is a\" type of its superclass, implemented in Java using \"extends\" (for classes) or \"implements\" (for interfaces). HAS-A represents composition/aggregation \u2014 a class contains a reference to another class as a field, meaning it \"has a\" relationship rather than being a specialized type of it. IS-A promotes polymorphism, while HAS-A promotes code reuse through delegation and is generally favored over deep inheritance hierarchies (\"favor composition over inheritance\") because it's more flexible and avoids tight coupling.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// IS-A\nclass Animal {\n}\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n}\n// Dog IS-A Animal\n// HAS-A\nclass Engine {\n}\nclass Car {\n    private Engine engine;\n    // Car HAS-A Engine (composition)\n}\n```",
