    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nCoupling refers to the degree of dependency between two classes/modules. Tight coupling means a class directly creates or depends heavily on the concrete implementation of another class, so changes in one force changes in the other, and the classes are hard to test or reuse independently. Loose coupling means classes depend on abstractions (interfaces) rather than concrete implementations, typically achieved via dependency injection, so implementations can be swapped without modifying the dependent class \u2014 this makes systems more maintainable, testable (easy to mock dependencies), and extensible.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Tight coupling\nclass NotificationService {\n    private EmailSender sender = new EmailSender();\n    // hardcoded, tightly coupled\n}\n// Loose coupling\nclass NotificationService {\n    private MessageSender sender;\n    // depends on an\n    interface NotificationService(MessageSender sender) {\n        this.sender = sender;\n    }\n    // injected - can be EmailSender, SMSSender, etc.\n}\n```",
