    explanation: "This question evaluates whether you can distinguish the alternatives in compare interfaces and abstract classes instead of listing definitions. A strong answer should compare their behavior, explain when each is appropriate, mention important limitations, and use a small Java example or concrete scenario where it clarifies the difference.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nAn abstract class can have both abstract methods (no body) and concrete methods (with implementation), can maintain state via instance fields, can have constructors, and supports single inheritance only (a class extends one abstract class). An interface (pre-Java 8) could only declare abstract methods and constants; since Java 8+, interfaces can also have default and static methods with bodies, but they still cannot hold instance state (no instance fields other than implicit public static final constants), and a class can implement multiple interfaces, giving more flexibility for multiple-inheritance-like behavior. Use an abstract class when classes share a common base implementation and state; use an interface when you need to define a contract that unrelated classes can implement, or when you need multiple inheritance of type.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nabstract class Shape {\n    abstract double area();\n    // must be implemented by subclass void describe() { System.out.println(\"This is a shape\");\n}\n// concrete, shared\n}\ninterface Drawable {\n    void draw();\n    // contract default void highlight() { System.out.println(\"Highlighted\");\n}\n// default method\n}\nclass Circle extends Shape implements Drawable {\n    double area() {\n        return 3.14 * 5 * 5;\n    }\n    public void draw() {\n        System.out.println(\"Drawing circle\");\n    }\n}\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nAn abstract class can have both abstract methods (no body) and concrete methods (with implementation), can maintain state via instance fields, can have constructors, and supports single inheritance only (a class extends one abstract class). An interface (pre-Java 8) could only declare abstract methods and constants; since Java 8+, interfaces can also have default and static methods with bodies, but they still cannot hold instance state (no instance fields other than implicit public static final constants), and a class can implement multiple interfaces, giving more flexibility for multiple-inheritance-like behavior. Use an abstract class when classes share a common base implementation and state; use an interface when you need to define a contract that unrelated classes can implement, or when you need multiple inheritance of type.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nabstract class Shape {\n    abstract double area();\n    // must be implemented by subclass void describe() { System.out.println(\"This is a shape\");\n}\n// concrete, shared\n}\ninterface Drawable {\n    void draw();\n    // contract default void highlight() { System.out.println(\"Highlighted\");\n}\n// default method\n}\nclass Circle extends Shape implements Drawable {\n    double area() {\n        return 3.14 * 5 * 5;\n    }\n    public void draw() {\n        System.out.println(\"Drawing circle\");\n    }\n}\n```",
