    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nSynchronization is Java's mechanism for controlling access to shared resources by multiple threads to prevent race conditions and ensure data consistency. When a method or block is marked synchronized, only one thread can execute that code (on the same lock/monitor) at a time \u2014 other threads must wait until the lock is released. This is essential when multiple threads read and write shared mutable state concurrently, as unsynchronized access can lead to inconsistent or corrupted data due to interleaved execution.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nclass BankAccount {\n    private double balance = 1000;\n    public synchronized void withdraw(double amount) {\n        if (balance >= amount) {\n            balance -= amount;\n            // without synchronized, two threads could both pass the check\n            // before either deducts, causing an incorrect final balance\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
