    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nHashMap stores key-value pairs in an internal array of \"buckets.\" When you call put(key, value), Java computes key.hashCode(), then applies an internal hash-spreading function to reduce collisions, and uses (n-1) & hash (where n is the array length, a power of 2) to determine the bucket index. If two keys hash to the same bucket (a collision), Java (since Java 8) stores multiple entries in that bucket as a linked list; if a single bucket accumulates 8 or more entries (and the table has at least 64 buckets), that bucket converts into a balanced red-black tree for O(log n) worst-case lookup instead of O(n) with a linked list. When retrieving a value, HashMap computes the hash, finds the bucket, then uses equals() to find the exact matching key within that bucket (since multiple different keys can share the same bucket).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nMap<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();\nmap.put(\"apple\", 1);\nmap.put(\"grape\", 2);\n// suppose \"apple\" and \"grape\" hash to the same bucket index\n// Both entries are stored in that bucket's linked list (or tree\nif bucket grows large);\n// map.get(\"grape\") walks the bucket and uses \"grape\".equals(storedKey) to find the right entry.\n```",
