    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nChecked exceptions are subclasses of Exception other than RuntimeException and its subclasses. The compiler requires them to be caught or declared with throws. IOException is a common example. Unchecked exceptions include RuntimeException and its subclasses, and Error; the compiler does not require them to be caught or declared. The distinction is about compile-time checking, not a guarantee that one category is always recoverable and the other is always a programming bug.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Checked - must handle\npublic void readConfig() throws FileNotFoundException {\n    new FileReader(\"config.txt\");\n}\n// Unchecked - optional to handle\npublic int divide(int a, int b) {\n    return a / b;\n    // ArithmeticException possible but not required to declare\n}\n```",
