    explanation: "This question evaluates your understanding of what garbage collectors are you familiar with in Java. A strong answer should define the concept precisely, explain how it behaves in practice, distinguish it from commonly confused concepts, and give a concrete example or consequence that shows you understand it rather than memorizing a definition.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThe JVM offers several garbage collector implementations, each with different trade-offs between throughput, latency, and memory footprint. Serial GC is a simple single-threaded collector suited for small applications with limited memory. Parallel GC (throughput collector) uses multiple threads for garbage collection to maximize throughput, at the cost of longer pause times, and was the default in older JVM versions. CMS (Concurrent Mark Sweep) aimed to minimize pause times by doing most collection work concurrently with the application, but is deprecated. G1 (Garbage-First) GC, the default since Java 9, divides the heap into regions and prioritizes collecting regions with the most garbage first, balancing throughput and pause time for medium-to-large heaps. ZGC and Shenandoah are newer low-latency collectors designed to keep pause times extremely short (sub-millisecond to a few milliseconds) even on very large heaps (multi-terabyte), by doing almost all work concurrently with application threads.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```text\n# Selecting a garbage collector via JVM flags\njava -XX:+UseG1GC -jar app.jar\n# G1, default modern choice\njava -XX:+UseZGC -jar app.jar\n# ZGC, for ultra-low-latency needs\njava -XX:+UseParallelGC -jar app.jar\n# Parallel, for batch/throughput-oriented jobs\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThe JVM offers several garbage collector implementations, each with different trade-offs between throughput, latency, and memory footprint. Serial GC is a simple single-threaded collector suited for small applications with limited memory. Parallel GC (throughput collector) uses multiple threads for garbage collection to maximize throughput, at the cost of longer pause times, and was the default in older JVM versions. CMS (Concurrent Mark Sweep) aimed to minimize pause times by doing most collection work concurrently with the application, but is deprecated. G1 (Garbage-First) GC, the default since Java 9, divides the heap into regions and prioritizes collecting regions with the most garbage first, balancing throughput and pause time for medium-to-large heaps. ZGC and Shenandoah are newer low-latency collectors designed to keep pause times extremely short (sub-millisecond to a few milliseconds) even on very large heaps (multi-terabyte), by doing almost all work concurrently with application threads.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```text\n# Selecting a garbage collector via JVM flags\njava -XX:+UseG1GC -jar app.jar\n# G1, default modern choice\njava -XX:+UseZGC -jar app.jar\n# ZGC, for ultra-low-latency needs\njava -XX:+UseParallelGC -jar app.jar\n# Parallel, for batch/throughput-oriented jobs\n```",
