    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA daemon thread is a background thread that does not prevent the JVM from exiting. When all non-daemon threads finish, the JVM can terminate even if daemon threads are still running, so daemon threads should not be used for work that must be completed or for critical cleanup. \"Daemon\" describes the thread's JVM shutdown behavior, not its priority. You must call setDaemon(true) before start().\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nThread backgroundLogger = new Thread(() -> {\n    while (true) {\n        System.out.println(\"Logging heartbeat...\"); try {\n            Thread.sleep(1000);\n        }\n        catch (InterruptedException e) {\n        }\n    }\n}\n);\nbackgroundLogger.setDaemon(true);\n// JVM won't wait\nfor this thread to finish backgroundLogger.start();\n// Main thread finishes its work and exits - JVM terminates, killing backgroundLogger too\n```",
