    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nORM (Object-Relational Mapping) is a programming technique that maps objects in an object-oriented language to rows in relational database tables, and object fields to table columns, letting developers interact with the database using familiar object-oriented code instead of writing raw SQL. ORM frameworks (like Hibernate, JPA) handle the translation of CRUD operations, manage relationships (one-to-many, many-to-many) as object references, provide caching, and abstract away database-specific SQL dialect differences, though they can introduce their own learning curve and, if misused, cause performance issues like the N+1 query problem.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\n// Without ORM (raw JDBC)\n  String sql = \"SELECT *\n  FROM employees\n  WHERE id = ?\";\n  // ... manual ResultSet mapping to an Employee object\n  // With ORM (Hibernate/JPA)\n  Employee emp = entityManager.find(Employee.class, 1L);\n  // object returned directly, SQL generated automatically\n```",
