    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nDependency Injection (DI) is a design pattern where an object's dependencies (the other objects it needs to function) are provided (\"injected\") from an external source rather than the object creating them itself. This is a specific implementation of the broader Inversion of Control (IoC) principle. DI can happen via constructor injection (dependencies passed through the constructor \u2014 generally preferred because it makes dependencies explicit and allows fields to be final/immutable), setter injection (dependencies set via setter methods, useful for optional dependencies), or field injection (dependencies injected directly into fields, common but discouraged for testability reasons). Spring's IoC container manages this automatically based on annotations like @Autowired or constructor-based wiring.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Without DI - tightly coupled, hard to test\nclass OrderService {\n    private PaymentGateway gateway = new StripeGateway();\n    // hardcoded dependency\n}\n// With DI (constructor injection) - loosely coupled, testable\nclass OrderService {\n    private final PaymentGateway gateway;\n    public OrderService(PaymentGateway gateway) {\n        // Spring injects the implementation this.gateway = gateway;\n    }\n}\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nDependency Injection (DI) is a design pattern where an object's dependencies (the other objects it needs to function) are provided (\"injected\") from an external source rather than the object creating them itself. This is a specific implementation of the broader Inversion of Control (IoC) principle. DI can happen via constructor injection (dependencies passed through the constructor \u2014 generally preferred because it makes dependencies explicit and allows fields to be final/immutable), setter injection (dependencies set via setter methods, useful for optional dependencies), or field injection (dependencies injected directly into fields, common but discouraged for testability reasons). Spring's IoC container manages this automatically based on annotations like @Autowired or constructor-based wiring.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Without DI - tightly coupled, hard to test\nclass OrderService {\n    private PaymentGateway gateway = new StripeGateway();\n    // hardcoded dependency\n}\n// With DI (constructor injection) - loosely coupled, testable\nclass OrderService {\n    private final PaymentGateway gateway;\n    public OrderService(PaymentGateway gateway) {\n        // Spring injects the implementation this.gateway = gateway;\n    }\n}\n```",
