    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA circular dependency occurs when two or more beans depend on each other, directly or indirectly, forming a cycle (Bean A needs Bean B, and Bean B needs Bean A). In Spring, this can cause a BeanCurrentlyInCreationException at startup, especially with constructor injection, because Spring cannot fully construct A without B already existing, and vice versa. Spring can sometimes resolve circular dependencies automatically for setter/field injection (via a mechanism using early bean references from the singleton cache), but constructor-based circular dependencies fail outright. The recommended fix is to redesign the classes to break the cycle \u2014 often by introducing a third class/interface that both depend on, using setter injection instead of constructor injection, or using @Lazy to defer initialization.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@Service class ServiceA {\n    @Autowired ServiceB serviceB;\n    // A depends on B\n}\n@Service class ServiceB {\n    @Autowired ServiceA serviceA;\n    // B depends on A - circular!\n}\n// Fix: extract shared logic into a ServiceC that both A and B depend on instead of depending on each other,\n// or use\n@Lazy on one of the\n@Autowired fields to defer resolution.\n```",
