    explanation: "This question evaluates whether you can distinguish the alternatives in compare factory and abstract factory instead of listing definitions. A strong answer should compare their behavior, explain when each is appropriate, mention important limitations, and use a small Java example or concrete scenario where it clarifies the difference.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThe Factory Method pattern defines a single method (often in a base class or interface) that subclasses override to create objects of a specific type \u2014 it deals with creating one family/hierarchy of related objects through inheritance. The Abstract Factory pattern is a \"factory of factories\" \u2014 it provides an interface for creating families of related or dependent objects without specifying their concrete classes, typically composing multiple factory methods together. Abstract Factory is used when you need to create groups of related objects that must be used together (e.g., a UI toolkit needing matching Button, Checkbox, and ScrollBar for either a Windows or Mac theme), while Factory Method is simpler and focuses on one product type at a time.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Factory Method - creates one product type\ninterface ShapeFactory {\n    Shape createShape();\n}\nclass CircleFactory implements ShapeFactory {\n    public Shape createShape() {\n        return new Circle();\n    }\n}\n// Abstract Factory - creates families of related products\ninterface UIFactory {\n    Button createButton();\n    Checkbox createCheckbox();\n}\nclass WindowsUIFactory implements UIFactory {\n    public Button createButton() {\n        return new WindowsButton();\n    }\n    public Checkbox createCheckbox() {\n        return new WindowsCheckbox();\n    }\n}\nclass MacUIFactory implements UIFactory {\n    public Button createButton() {\n        return new MacButton();\n    }\n    public Checkbox createCheckbox() {\n        return new MacCheckbox();\n    }\n}\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThe Factory Method pattern defines a single method (often in a base class or interface) that subclasses override to create objects of a specific type \u2014 it deals with creating one family/hierarchy of related objects through inheritance. The Abstract Factory pattern is a \"factory of factories\" \u2014 it provides an interface for creating families of related or dependent objects without specifying their concrete classes, typically composing multiple factory methods together. Abstract Factory is used when you need to create groups of related objects that must be used together (e.g., a UI toolkit needing matching Button, Checkbox, and ScrollBar for either a Windows or Mac theme), while Factory Method is simpler and focuses on one product type at a time.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Factory Method - creates one product type\ninterface ShapeFactory {\n    Shape createShape();\n}\nclass CircleFactory implements ShapeFactory {\n    public Shape createShape() {\n        return new Circle();\n    }\n}\n// Abstract Factory - creates families of related products\ninterface UIFactory {\n    Button createButton();\n    Checkbox createCheckbox();\n}\nclass WindowsUIFactory implements UIFactory {\n    public Button createButton() {\n        return new WindowsButton();\n    }\n    public Checkbox createCheckbox() {\n        return new WindowsCheckbox();\n    }\n}\nclass MacUIFactory implements UIFactory {\n    public Button createButton() {\n        return new MacButton();\n    }\n    public Checkbox createCheckbox() {\n        return new MacCheckbox();\n    }\n}\n```",
