    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nConstructor injection provides dependencies through a class's constructor at object creation time; it makes dependencies mandatory and explicit, allows fields to be declared \"final\" (immutable), and ensures the object is always in a fully valid state right after construction \u2014 this is why the Spring team recommends it as the default choice. Setter injection provides dependencies through public setter methods after object construction; it's useful for optional dependencies or when you need to change a dependency later, but it means the object could exist temporarily in an incomplete/invalid state before all setters are called, and fields can't be final.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Constructor injection\nclass OrderService {\n    private final PaymentGateway gateway;\n    // can be final - immutable after construction\n    public OrderService(PaymentGateway gateway) {\n        this.gateway = gateway;\n    }\n}\n// Setter injection\nclass OrderService {\n    private PaymentGateway gateway;\n    // cannot be final\n    public void setGateway(PaymentGateway gateway) {\n        this.gateway = gateway;\n    }\n    // optional, can be reassigned\n}\n```",
