    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nAccess specifiers (also called access modifiers) control the visibility/accessibility of classes, methods, and fields from other parts of a program. Java has four levels: private (accessible only within the same class), default/package-private (no keyword \u2014 accessible within the same package only), protected (accessible within the same package plus subclasses even in different packages), and public (accessible from anywhere). Choosing the right access level supports encapsulation by exposing only what's necessary and hiding implementation details.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\npublic class BankAccount {\n    private double balance;\n    // only this\n    class can access directly protected String accountType;\n    // this\n    class, package, and subclasses String branchCode;\n    // default - same package only\n    public String accountNumber;\n    // accessible from anywhere\n}\n```",
