    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nSpring Boot \"starters\" are convenient dependency descriptors (Maven/Gradle artifacts) that bundle together a curated set of commonly-used, version-compatible libraries for a particular type of functionality, eliminating the need to manually figure out and manage individual dependency versions. For example, spring-boot-starter-web pulls in Spring MVC, an embedded Tomcat server, Jackson for JSON, and validation libraries all at once, with versions guaranteed to work together (managed by Spring Boot's parent BOM). This drastically simplifies project setup and avoids \"dependency hell.\"\n\n**Example:**\n\n```xml\n<!-- pom.xml -->\n<dependency>\n<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>\n<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>\n</dependency>\n<dependency>\n<groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>\n<artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>\n</dependency>\n<!-- Adding these two starters gives you a full web + database stack with compatible versions -->\n```",
