    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nHibernate's first-level cache is tied to the current persistence context/Session and is enabled by default. If the same entity is loaded by identifier within that context, Hibernate can return the already-managed instance without another database lookup. A second-level cache is optional and shared across persistence contexts through a configured Hibernate-compatible cache solution; it can help with data that is read frequently and changes infrequently. Hibernate also has a query-cache feature, but it should be enabled deliberately because cache invalidation and query-result staleness can make it unsuitable for many workloads.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// First-level cache (automatic)\nEmployee e1 = session.get(Employee.class, 1L);\n// hits DB\nEmployee e2 = session.get(Employee.class, 1L);\n// returns from session cache, no DB hit\n// Second-level cache (requires config)\n@Entity\n@Cacheable\n@org.hibernate.annotations.Cache(usage = CacheConcurrencyStrategy.READ_WRITE) public class Country {\n    // rarely-changing reference data - good candidate\n    for L2 cache\n    @Id Long id;\n    String name;\n}\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nHibernate's first-level cache is tied to the current persistence context/Session and is enabled by default. If the same entity is loaded by identifier within that context, Hibernate can return the already-managed instance without another database lookup. A second-level cache is optional and shared across persistence contexts through a configured Hibernate-compatible cache solution; it can help with data that is read frequently and changes infrequently. Hibernate also has a query-cache feature, but it should be enabled deliberately because cache invalidation and query-result staleness can make it unsuitable for many workloads.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// First-level cache (automatic)\nEmployee e1 = session.get(Employee.class, 1L);\n// hits DB\nEmployee e2 = session.get(Employee.class, 1L);\n// returns from session cache, no DB hit\n// Second-level cache (requires config)\n@Entity\n@Cacheable\n@org.hibernate.annotations.Cache(usage = CacheConcurrencyStrategy.READ_WRITE) public class Country {\n    // rarely-changing reference data - good candidate\n    for L2 cache\n    @Id Long id;\n    String name;\n}\n```",
