    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA common SQL approach uses a window function like DENSE_RANK() (which handles ties correctly without skipping ranks) to rank salaries in descending order, then filters for the desired rank in an outer query. An older, more portable approach (works on databases without window function support) uses a correlated subquery counting distinct higher salaries.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\n-- Using window function (modern SQL, e.g. PostgreSQL, MySQL 8+)\n\nSELECT salary\n  FROM (\n\nSELECT salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk\n  FROM employees ) ranked\n  WHERE rnk = 3;\n\n-- 3rd highest salary\n\n-- Portable subquery approach\n\nSELECT DISTINCT salary\n  FROM employees e1\n  WHERE 2 = (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT salary)\n  FROM employees e2\n  WHERE e2.salary >= e1.salary);\n\n-- Also finds the 3rd highest by counting how many distinct salaries are >= each salary\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA common SQL approach uses a window function like DENSE_RANK() (which handles ties correctly without skipping ranks) to rank salaries in descending order, then filters for the desired rank in an outer query. An older, more portable approach (works on databases without window function support) uses a correlated subquery counting distinct higher salaries.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\n-- Using window function (modern SQL, e.g. PostgreSQL, MySQL 8+)\n\nSELECT salary\n  FROM (\n\nSELECT salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk\n  FROM employees ) ranked\n  WHERE rnk = 3;\n\n-- 3rd highest salary\n\n-- Portable subquery approach\n\nSELECT DISTINCT salary\n  FROM employees e1\n  WHERE 2 = (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT salary)\n  FROM employees e2\n  WHERE e2.salary >= e1.salary);\n\n-- Also finds the 3rd highest by counting how many distinct salaries are >= each salary\n```",
