    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\n@ComponentScan tells Spring which packages to scan for classes annotated with stereotype annotations like @Component, @Service, @Repository, and @Controller, so it can automatically detect and register them as beans in the application context, without needing to manually declare each bean in XML or Java config. By default, @SpringBootApplication (which includes @ComponentScan) scans the package of the main application class and all its sub-packages, which is why Spring Boot projects are conventionally structured with the main class at the root package.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@SpringBootApplication\n// includes\n@ComponentScan(basePackages = \"com.example.myapp\") implicitly\n@ComponentScan(basePackages = {\n    \"com.example.myapp\", \"com.example.sharedlib\"\n}\n)\n// explicit, scans extra package too\npublic class MyApplication {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        SpringApplication.run(MyApplication.class, args);\n    }\n}\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\n@ComponentScan tells Spring which packages to scan for classes annotated with stereotype annotations like @Component, @Service, @Repository, and @Controller, so it can automatically detect and register them as beans in the application context, without needing to manually declare each bean in XML or Java config. By default, @SpringBootApplication (which includes @ComponentScan) scans the package of the main application class and all its sub-packages, which is why Spring Boot projects are conventionally structured with the main class at the root package.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@SpringBootApplication\n// includes\n@ComponentScan(basePackages = \"com.example.myapp\") implicitly\n@ComponentScan(basePackages = {\n    \"com.example.myapp\", \"com.example.sharedlib\"\n}\n)\n// explicit, scans extra package too\npublic class MyApplication {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        SpringApplication.run(MyApplication.class, args);\n    }\n}\n```",
