    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nQuery optimization involves several strategies: (1) Add proper indexes on columns used in WHERE, JOIN, and ORDER BY clauses, since indexes let the database avoid full table scans; (2) Avoid SELECT * \u2014 fetch only needed columns to reduce I/O; (3) Use EXPLAIN/EXPLAIN ANALYZE to inspect the query execution plan and identify full table scans, missing indexes, or inefficient joins; (4) Avoid N+1 query problems in ORMs by using JOIN FETCH or batch fetching; (5) Use pagination (LIMIT/OFFSET or keyset pagination) instead of loading entire large tables; (6) Denormalize selectively or use materialized views for read-heavy reporting queries; (7) Use connection pooling to avoid connection setup overhead; (8) Cache frequently-read, rarely-changed data; (9) Rewrite correlated subqueries as joins where possible, since joins are often optimized better by the query planner.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\n-- Before: full table scan, slow\n\nSELECT *\n  FROM orders\n  WHERE customer_email = 'john@example.com';\n\n-- After: add an index,\n\nselect only needed columns CREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer_email\n  ON orders(customer_email);\n\nSELECT order_id, order_date, total\n  FROM orders\n  WHERE customer_email = 'john@example.com';\n\n-- Use EXPLAIN to confirm the index is now used instead of a sequential scan.\n```",
