    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThe general best practice is to NOT test private methods directly \u2014 instead, test them indirectly through the public methods that call them, since private methods are implementation details and the public API is the actual contract you care about. If a private method is complex enough to need direct/isolated testing, that's often a sign it should be extracted into its own class or made package-private so it can be unit tested directly. In rare cases where direct testing of a private method is unavoidable (e.g., legacy code you can't refactor), reflection can be used to invoke it, though this is generally discouraged.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nclass Calculator {\n    public int computeDiscountedPrice(int price, int quantity) {\n        return applyDiscount(price * quantity);\n        //\n        private method exercised indirectly\n    }\n    private int applyDiscount(int total) {\n        return total > 100 ? (int)(total * 0.9) : total;\n    }\n}\n// Test the\npublic method, which exercises the private method's logic:\n@Test void testComputeDiscountedPrice() { Calculator calc = new Calculator(); assertEquals(90, calc.computeDiscountedPrice(20, 10));\n// indirectly verifies applyDiscount logic\n\n }\n```",
