    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA systematic approach includes: (1) Centralized logging (e.g., ELK stack, Splunk) with structured logs and correlation/trace IDs to follow a request across services; (2) Application Performance Monitoring (APM) tools like New Relic, Dynatrace, or Datadog to see latency, error rates, and traces; (3) Metrics dashboards (Prometheus + Grafana) tracking key indicators like error rate, response time, throughput; (4) Alerting on anomalies (spike in 5xx errors, latency degradation); (5) Distributed tracing (e.g., Zipkin, Jaeger, OpenTelemetry) to pinpoint which microservice or call in a chain is failing or slow; (6) Reproducing the issue in a staging environment using the same inputs; (7) Reviewing recent deployments/changes as the first suspect (most production incidents follow a recent change); (8) Using heap/thread dumps for memory or concurrency-related issues.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```text\n// Adding a correlation/trace ID to logs\nfor tracing a request across microservices MDC.put(\"traceId\", UUID.randomUUID().toString());\nlog.info(\"Processing order {}\", orderId);\n// Then in Kibana/Splunk, search by traceId to see the full request lifecycle across all services,\n// pinpointing exactly where in the chain an error or slowdown occurred.\n```",
