    explanation: "This question evaluates your understanding of design a database for a student attendance system in Java. A strong answer should define the concept precisely, explain how it behaves in practice, distinguish it from commonly confused concepts, and give a concrete example or consequence that shows you understand it rather than memorizing a definition.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nCore entities: Student (id, name, roll_number, class_id), Class (id, name, section), Subject (id, name), Teacher (id, name), and Attendance (id, student_id FK, subject_id FK, date, status [PRESENT/ABSENT/LATE], marked_by_teacher_id FK). The relationships: a Student belongs to a Class (many-to-one), a Class has many Subjects (many-to-many via a ClassSubject table, or simplified), and Attendance is a junction/fact table capturing daily attendance per student per subject, with a unique constraint on (student_id, subject_id, date) to prevent duplicate entries for the same day.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE student (id INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(100), roll_number VARCHAR(20), class_id INT);\n\nCREATE TABLE subject (id INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(50));\n\nCREATE TABLE attendance ( id INT PRIMARY KEY, student_id INT REFERENCES student(id), subject_id INT REFERENCES subject(id), date DATE, status VARCHAR(10), UNIQUE(student_id, subject_id, date) );\n\n-- Query: attendance percentage per student for a subject\n\nSELECT student_id, SUM(CASE WHEN status='PRESENT' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) * 100.0 / COUNT(*) AS attendance_pct\n  FROM attendance\n  WHERE subject_id = 1\n  GROUP BY student_id;\n```",
