    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\n@Controller is the traditional Spring MVC annotation used for classes whose methods typically return view names (like JSP or Thymeleaf template names) that get resolved and rendered as HTML pages \u2014 if you want a method in a @Controller to return raw data (like JSON) instead of a view, you must additionally annotate that method with @ResponseBody. @RestController is a convenience annotation that combines @Controller and @ResponseBody at the class level, so every method's return value is automatically serialized (typically to JSON) and written directly to the HTTP response body \u2014 this is the standard choice for building REST APIs.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@Controller public class WebController {\n    @GetMapping(\"/home\") public String home() {\n        return \"home\";\n    }\n    // returns a view name \"home.html\" to render\n    @GetMapping(\"/data\")\n    @ResponseBody\n    // needed here to\n    return raw JSON instead of a view public User getData() {\n        return new User(\"Alice\");\n    }\n}\n@RestController\n// every method behaves like it has\n@ResponseBody public class ApiController {\n    @GetMapping(\"/api/user\") public User getUser() {\n        return new User(\"Alice\");\n    }\n    // serialized directly to JSON\n}\n```",
