    explanation: "This question evaluates your understanding of how do spring mvc request-mapping annotations work in Java. A strong answer should define the concept precisely, explain how it behaves in practice, distinguish it from commonly confused concepts, and give a concrete example or consequence that shows you understand it rather than memorizing a definition.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nSpring MVC \"mappings\" refer to annotations that map incoming HTTP requests to specific controller handler methods based on URL path, HTTP method, headers, or parameters. @RequestMapping is the general-purpose base annotation (can specify method=RequestMethod.GET, etc.), while Spring provides more concise shortcut annotations for common HTTP verbs: @GetMapping, @PostMapping, @PutMapping, @DeleteMapping, and @PatchMapping. You can also bind parts of the URL or request to method parameters using @PathVariable (for URI template variables), @RequestParam (for query string parameters), and @RequestBody (for deserializing the JSON/XML request body into a Java object).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@RestController\n@RequestMapping(\"/api/users\")\n// base path\nfor all methods in this controller public class UserController {\n    @GetMapping(\"/{id}\")\n    // maps\n    GET /api/users/5\n    public User getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {\n        ...\n    }\n    @GetMapping\n    // maps\n    GET /api/users?active=true\n    public List<User> getUsers(@RequestParam boolean active) {\n        ...\n    }\n    @PostMapping\n    // maps POST /api/users with JSON body\n    public User createUser(@RequestBody User user) {\n        ...\n    }\n}\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nSpring MVC \"mappings\" refer to annotations that map incoming HTTP requests to specific controller handler methods based on URL path, HTTP method, headers, or parameters. @RequestMapping is the general-purpose base annotation (can specify method=RequestMethod.GET, etc.), while Spring provides more concise shortcut annotations for common HTTP verbs: @GetMapping, @PostMapping, @PutMapping, @DeleteMapping, and @PatchMapping. You can also bind parts of the URL or request to method parameters using @PathVariable (for URI template variables), @RequestParam (for query string parameters), and @RequestBody (for deserializing the JSON/XML request body into a Java object).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@RestController\n@RequestMapping(\"/api/users\")\n// base path\nfor all methods in this controller public class UserController {\n    @GetMapping(\"/{id}\")\n    // maps\n    GET /api/users/5\n    public User getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {\n        ...\n    }\n    @GetMapping\n    // maps\n    GET /api/users?active=true\n    public List<User> getUsers(@RequestParam boolean active) {\n        ...\n    }\n    @PostMapping\n    // maps POST /api/users with JSON body\n    public User createUser(@RequestBody User user) {\n        ...\n    }\n}\n```",
