    difficulty: Difficulty.MEDIUM,
    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "A scenario-based Data Structures & Algorithms interview question about how do you generate all permutations of a string in java, focusing on the decisions, trade-offs, failure cases, and practical steps a strong Java candidate should explain.",
    explanation: "This question evaluates how you would handle how do you generate all permutations of a string in java in a real Java application. A strong answer should make the assumptions explicit, explain the design or troubleshooting sequence, identify failure modes and trade-offs, and finish with how you would validate the result in production.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nGenerating all permutations of a string is a classic backtracking problem. The idea is to fix one character at a time at the current position, recursively permute the remaining characters, then swap back (backtrack) to try the next character at that position. For a string of length n, there are n! permutations, so this approach has exponential time complexity, which is expected for this class of problem.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\npublic static void permute(char[] arr, int start) {\n    if (start == arr.length - 1) {\n        System.out.println(new String(arr));\n        return;\n    }\n    for (int i = start; i < arr.length; i++) {\n        swap(arr, start, i);\n        permute(arr, start + 1);\n        // recurse on the rest swap(arr, start, i);\n        // backtrack\n    }\n}\nprivate static void swap(char[] arr, int i, int j) {\n    char temp = arr[i];\n    arr[i] = arr[j];\n    arr[j] = temp;\n}\n// permute(\"ABC\".toCharArray(), 0) prints: ABC, ACB, BAC, BCA, CBA, CAB\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nGenerating all permutations of a string is a classic backtracking problem. The idea is to fix one character at a time at the current position, recursively permute the remaining characters, then swap back (backtrack) to try the next character at that position. For a string of length n, there are n! permutations, so this approach has exponential time complexity, which is expected for this class of problem.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\npublic static void permute(char[] arr, int start) {\n    if (start == arr.length - 1) {\n        System.out.println(new String(arr));\n        return;\n    }\n    for (int i = start; i < arr.length; i++) {\n        swap(arr, start, i);\n        permute(arr, start + 1);\n        // recurse on the rest swap(arr, start, i);\n        // backtrack\n    }\n}\nprivate static void swap(char[] arr, int i, int j) {\n    char temp = arr[i];\n    arr[i] = arr[j];\n    arr[j] = temp;\n}\n// permute(\"ABC\".toCharArray(), 0) prints: ABC, ACB, BAC, BCA, CBA, CAB\n```",
