    explanation: "This question evaluates your understanding of common http status codes in Java. A strong answer should define the concept precisely, explain how it behaves in practice, distinguish it from commonly confused concepts, and give a concrete example or consequence that shows you understand it rather than memorizing a definition.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nHTTP status codes are grouped by their first digit. 2xx (Success): 200 OK (successful GET/PUT), 201 Created (successful POST that created a resource), 204 No Content (successful request with no response body, common for DELETE). 3xx (Redirection): 301 Moved Permanently, 304 Not Modified (used with caching). 4xx (Client Error): 400 Bad Request (malformed request), 401 Unauthorized (missing/invalid authentication), 403 Forbidden (authenticated but not authorized), 404 Not Found, 409 Conflict (e.g., duplicate resource), 429 Too Many Requests (rate limiting). 5xx (Server Error): 500 Internal Server Error (unhandled exception), 502 Bad Gateway, 503 Service Unavailable (server overloaded or down for maintenance).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Returning proper status codes in Spring\n@PostMapping(\"/users\") public ResponseEntity<User> createUser(@RequestBody User user) {\n    User saved = userService.save(user);\n    return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED).body(saved);\n    // 201 Created\n}\n@GetMapping(\"/users/{id}\") public ResponseEntity<User> getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {\n    return userService.findById(id) .map(ResponseEntity::ok)\n    // 200 OK .orElse(ResponseEntity.notFound().build());\n    // 404 Not Found\n}\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nHTTP status codes are grouped by their first digit. 2xx (Success): 200 OK (successful GET/PUT), 201 Created (successful POST that created a resource), 204 No Content (successful request with no response body, common for DELETE). 3xx (Redirection): 301 Moved Permanently, 304 Not Modified (used with caching). 4xx (Client Error): 400 Bad Request (malformed request), 401 Unauthorized (missing/invalid authentication), 403 Forbidden (authenticated but not authorized), 404 Not Found, 409 Conflict (e.g., duplicate resource), 429 Too Many Requests (rate limiting). 5xx (Server Error): 500 Internal Server Error (unhandled exception), 502 Bad Gateway, 503 Service Unavailable (server overloaded or down for maintenance).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Returning proper status codes in Spring\n@PostMapping(\"/users\") public ResponseEntity<User> createUser(@RequestBody User user) {\n    User saved = userService.save(user);\n    return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED).body(saved);\n    // 201 Created\n}\n@GetMapping(\"/users/{id}\") public ResponseEntity<User> getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {\n    return userService.findById(id) .map(ResponseEntity::ok)\n    // 200 OK .orElse(ResponseEntity.notFound().build());\n    // 404 Not Found\n}\n```",
