    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\n401 Unauthorized means the request lacks valid authentication credentials altogether \u2014 the server doesn't know who you are (or your credentials are invalid/expired), so it's asking you to authenticate (log in, provide a valid token). 403 Forbidden means the server DOES know who you are (you're successfully authenticated), but you don't have permission to access the requested resource \u2014 it's an authorization failure, not an authentication one. A helpful mnemonic: 401 = \"who are you?\", 403 = \"I know who you are, but you can't do that.\"\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// 401 - no/invalid token provided\nGET /api/admin/reports Authorization: (missing or expired\ntoken) --> 401 Unauthorized\n// 403 - valid token, but user role lacks permission\nGET\n/api/admin/reports Authorization: Bearer <valid-token-for-a-regular-user> --> 403 Forbidden\n(this user is authenticated but is not an ADMIN)\n```",
