    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "A focused SQL & Databases interview question about database indexing, covering the core concept, how it behaves in Java, and the practical distinction an interviewer is likely to probe.",
    explanation: "This question evaluates your understanding of database indexing in Java. A strong answer should define the concept precisely, explain how it behaves in practice, distinguish it from commonly confused concepts, and give a concrete example or consequence that shows you understand it rather than memorizing a definition.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nAn index is a separate on-disk data structure (commonly a B-tree or B+tree) that the database maintains alongside a table, allowing it to look up rows matching a condition (like WHERE column = value) much faster than scanning every row (a full table scan). Indexes dramatically speed up SELECT queries, JOINs, and ORDER BY operations on indexed columns, but they come at a cost: every INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE must also update the index structures, so excessive indexing slows down writes and consumes additional disk space. Best practice is to index columns frequently used in WHERE clauses, JOIN conditions, and foreign keys, but avoid indexing every column blindly.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\n-- Without an index: full table scan across millions of rows\n\nSELECT *\n  FROM orders\n  WHERE customer_id = 12345;\n\n-- With an index: fast lookup via B-tree traversal\n\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer_id\n  ON orders(customer_id);\n\nSELECT *\n  FROM orders\n  WHERE customer_id = 12345;\n\n-- now uses the index, much faster\n```",
