    explanation: "This question evaluates whether you can distinguish the alternatives in compare acid and cap instead of listing definitions. A strong answer should compare their behavior, explain when each is appropriate, mention important limitations, and use a small Java example or concrete scenario where it clarifies the difference.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nACID (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability) is a set of guarantees traditional relational databases provide for transactions: a transaction fully completes or fully fails (Atomicity), the database moves from one valid state to another (Consistency), concurrent transactions don't interfere with each other (Isolation), and committed changes survive crashes (Durability). CAP theorem, on the other hand, is about distributed systems and states that a distributed data store can only guarantee two of the following three at the same time: Consistency (every read gets the latest write), Availability (every request gets a response, even if not the latest data), and Partition tolerance (the system continues to function despite network partitions between nodes). Since network partitions are unavoidable in real distributed systems, the practical trade-off is usually between Consistency and Availability (CP vs AP systems) \u2014 for example, traditional relational databases in single-node mode prioritize ACID consistency, while many NoSQL databases are designed as AP systems that favor availability with eventual consistency.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\n// ACID example - bank transfer either fully happens or not at all BEGIN TRANSACTION;\n\nUPDATE accounts\n  SET balance = balance - 100\n  WHERE id = 1;\n\nUPDATE accounts\n  SET balance = balance + 100\n  WHERE id = 2;\n  COMMIT;\n\n-- Atomicity ensures both updates succeed together or neither does\n  // CAP trade-off example\n  // A CP system (like a strongly-consistent config store, e.g. ZooKeeper/etcd) will refuse to serve\n  // reads/writes during a network partition rather than risk returning stale data.\n  // An AP system (like Cassandra in its default config) will keep serving requests during a partition,\n  // possibly returning slightly stale data until the partition heals and nodes reconcile.\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nACID (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability) is a set of guarantees traditional relational databases provide for transactions: a transaction fully completes or fully fails (Atomicity), the database moves from one valid state to another (Consistency), concurrent transactions don't interfere with each other (Isolation), and committed changes survive crashes (Durability). CAP theorem, on the other hand, is about distributed systems and states that a distributed data store can only guarantee two of the following three at the same time: Consistency (every read gets the latest write), Availability (every request gets a response, even if not the latest data), and Partition tolerance (the system continues to function despite network partitions between nodes). Since network partitions are unavoidable in real distributed systems, the practical trade-off is usually between Consistency and Availability (CP vs AP systems) \u2014 for example, traditional relational databases in single-node mode prioritize ACID consistency, while many NoSQL databases are designed as AP systems that favor availability with eventual consistency.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\n// ACID example - bank transfer either fully happens or not at all BEGIN TRANSACTION;\n\nUPDATE accounts\n  SET balance = balance - 100\n  WHERE id = 1;\n\nUPDATE accounts\n  SET balance = balance + 100\n  WHERE id = 2;\n  COMMIT;\n\n-- Atomicity ensures both updates succeed together or neither does\n  // CAP trade-off example\n  // A CP system (like a strongly-consistent config store, e.g. ZooKeeper/etcd) will refuse to serve\n  // reads/writes during a network partition rather than risk returning stale data.\n  // An AP system (like Cassandra in its default config) will keep serving requests during a partition,\n  // possibly returning slightly stale data until the partition heals and nodes reconcile.\n```",
