    explanation: "This question evaluates your understanding of inversion of control and dependency injection in Java. A strong answer should define the concept precisely, explain how it behaves in practice, distinguish it from commonly confused concepts, and give a concrete example or consequence that shows you understand it rather than memorizing a definition.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nInversion of Control (IoC) is a broad design principle where the control of object creation, configuration, and lifecycle is transferred from the application code itself to an external framework/container \u2014 instead of your code calling \"new\" to create dependencies and controlling the flow, the framework calls your code and provides what it needs (\"Don't call us, we'll call you\"). Dependency Injection is the most common technique used to implement IoC specifically for supplying an object's dependencies: rather than a class creating its own dependencies, the IoC container constructs and injects them. In Spring, the ApplicationContext acts as the IoC container, and DI is achieved via annotations like @Autowired or constructor injection.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Without IoC -\nclass controls its own dependency creation class OrderService {\n    private PaymentGateway gateway = new StripeGateway();\n    // OrderService is in control, tightly coupled\n}\n// With IoC + DI - Spring container is in control\n@Service class OrderService {\n    private final PaymentGateway gateway;\n    @Autowired\n    // Spring's IoC container creates and injects the dependency - control is inverted\n    public OrderService(PaymentGateway gateway) {\n        this.gateway = gateway;\n    }\n}\n```",
