    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nSetter injection (using setter methods to supply dependencies after object construction) is best suited for optional dependencies that have a sensible default and might be reconfigured later, since it allows the dependency to be changed after the object is created. This contrasts with constructor injection, which is preferred for mandatory dependencies since it guarantees the object can never exist in an incomplete state and allows immutable (final) fields, and field injection (using @Autowired directly on a field), which is the most concise but is generally discouraged because it hides dependencies (they're not visible in the constructor signature), makes unit testing harder (you can't easily pass mocks without reflection or a DI framework), and doesn't support immutability.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Setter injection - good\nfor optional dependency class ReportService {\n    private Logger logger;\n    // optional - has internal default behavior\n    if not set public void setLogger(Logger logger) {\n        this.logger = logger;\n    }\n}\n// Constructor injection - good\nfor mandatory dependency, testable, immutable class ReportService {\n    private final DataSource dataSource;\n    // mandatory - service can't function without it\n    public ReportService(DataSource dataSource) {\n        this.dataSource = dataSource;\n    }\n}\n// Field injection - concise but discouraged\nfor testability reasons class ReportService {\n    @Autowired private DataSource dataSource;\n    // hard to unit test without a Spring context or reflection\n}\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nSetter injection (using setter methods to supply dependencies after object construction) is best suited for optional dependencies that have a sensible default and might be reconfigured later, since it allows the dependency to be changed after the object is created. This contrasts with constructor injection, which is preferred for mandatory dependencies since it guarantees the object can never exist in an incomplete state and allows immutable (final) fields, and field injection (using @Autowired directly on a field), which is the most concise but is generally discouraged because it hides dependencies (they're not visible in the constructor signature), makes unit testing harder (you can't easily pass mocks without reflection or a DI framework), and doesn't support immutability.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Setter injection - good\nfor optional dependency class ReportService {\n    private Logger logger;\n    // optional - has internal default behavior\n    if not set public void setLogger(Logger logger) {\n        this.logger = logger;\n    }\n}\n// Constructor injection - good\nfor mandatory dependency, testable, immutable class ReportService {\n    private final DataSource dataSource;\n    // mandatory - service can't function without it\n    public ReportService(DataSource dataSource) {\n        this.dataSource = dataSource;\n    }\n}\n// Field injection - concise but discouraged\nfor testability reasons class ReportService {\n    @Autowired private DataSource dataSource;\n    // hard to unit test without a Spring context or reflection\n}\n```",
