    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThe Singleton pattern ensures that a class has only one instance throughout the application's lifetime and provides a single global access point to that instance. It's implemented by making the constructor private (so no other code can instantiate it directly) and providing a static method (commonly named getInstance()) that returns the single shared instance, creating it lazily or eagerly. In multi-threaded environments, care must be taken to make instance creation thread-safe (using synchronized, double-checked locking, or the \"initialization-on-demand holder\" idiom), or simply using an enum, which the JVM guarantees to be a thread-safe singleton by design. In Spring, beans are singleton-scoped by default, so the framework manages this pattern for you automatically without manual boilerplate.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Classic thread-safe singleton using double-checked locking\npublic class ConfigManager {\n    private static volatile ConfigManager instance;\n    private ConfigManager() {\n    }\n    //\n    private constructor prevents external instantiation public static ConfigManager getInstance() {\n        if (instance == null) {\n            synchronized (ConfigManager.class) {\n                if (instance == null) {\n                    instance = new ConfigManager();\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return instance;\n    }\n}\n// Simplest thread-safe singleton using an\nenum public enum ConfigManagerEnum {\n    INSTANCE;\n    public void doSomething() {\n        /* ... */\n    }\n}\n```",
