    explanation: "This question evaluates your understanding of what is the string pool in Java. A strong answer should define the concept precisely, explain how it behaves in practice, distinguish it from commonly confused concepts, and give a concrete example or consequence that shows you understand it rather than memorizing a definition.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThe String pool (also called the intern pool) is a special memory region within the JVM heap (in the Metaspace area since Java 7+, previously PermGen) where the JVM stores unique String literal values to avoid creating duplicate String objects. When you create a string using a literal (e.g., String s = \"hello\";), the JVM first checks the pool: if an identical string already exists there, it reuses that reference instead of creating a new object; if not, it adds the new literal to the pool. Strings created using \"new String(...)\" bypass the pool and always create a new object on the heap, though you can manually add them to the pool using the intern() method.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nString s1 = \"hello\";\n// added to string pool (or reused\nif already present) String s2 = \"hello\";\n// reuses the SAME pooled object as s1 System.out.println(s1 == s2);\n// true - same reference from the pool\nString s3 =\nnew String(\"hello\");\n// forces a\nnew object on the heap, bypassing the pool System.out.println(s1 == s3);\n// false - different object\n```",
