    shortDescription: "A focused Core Java & OOP interview question about why should hashcode() be consistent with equals(), explaining the underlying reason, practical impact, and the details that distinguish a strong Java answer.",
    explanation: "This question evaluates whether you understand why why should hashcode() be consistent with equals() exists or is used, not just its definition. A strong answer should explain the underlying mechanism, the practical benefit, the important limitation or trade-off, and a concise Java example when useful.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nJava's contract requires that if two objects are equal according to equals(), they MUST return the same hashCode() value (though the reverse isn't required \u2014 unequal objects can share a hash code, called a \"collision\"). This consistency is critical because hash-based collections like HashMap and HashSet use hashCode() to determine which bucket to place/look for an object in, and then use equals() to confirm an exact match within that bucket. If you override equals() without correspondingly overriding hashCode(), two \"equal\" objects could end up in different buckets, meaning a HashMap/HashSet would fail to recognize them as duplicates or fail to retrieve a value you just inserted.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nclass Point {\n    int x, y;\n    public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        if (!(o instanceof Point)) return false;\n        Point p = (Point) o;\n        return x == p.x && y == p.y;\n    }\n    // BUG:\n    if you forget to override hashCode() here consistently with equals(),\n    // two Points with the same x,y could get DIFFERENT default hash codes (based on memory address),\n    // so a HashSet<Point> could store \"duplicate\" points because they land in different buckets.\n    @Override public int hashCode() {\n        return Objects.hash(x, y);\n    }\n    // must be overridden together with equals()\n}\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nJava's contract requires that if two objects are equal according to equals(), they MUST return the same hashCode() value (though the reverse isn't required \u2014 unequal objects can share a hash code, called a \"collision\"). This consistency is critical because hash-based collections like HashMap and HashSet use hashCode() to determine which bucket to place/look for an object in, and then use equals() to confirm an exact match within that bucket. If you override equals() without correspondingly overriding hashCode(), two \"equal\" objects could end up in different buckets, meaning a HashMap/HashSet would fail to recognize them as duplicates or fail to retrieve a value you just inserted.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nclass Point {\n    int x, y;\n    public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        if (!(o instanceof Point)) return false;\n        Point p = (Point) o;\n        return x == p.x && y == p.y;\n    }\n    // BUG:\n    if you forget to override hashCode() here consistently with equals(),\n    // two Points with the same x,y could get DIFFERENT default hash codes (based on memory address),\n    // so a HashSet<Point> could store \"duplicate\" points because they land in different buckets.\n    @Override public int hashCode() {\n        return Objects.hash(x, y);\n    }\n    // must be overridden together with equals()\n}\n```",
