    shortDescription: "A focused Core Java & OOP interview question about how does hashmap work at a high level, covering the core concept, how it behaves in Java, and the practical distinction an interviewer is likely to probe.",
    explanation: "This question evaluates your understanding of how does hashmap work at a high level in Java. A strong answer should define the concept precisely, explain how it behaves in practice, distinguish it from commonly confused concepts, and give a concrete example or consequence that shows you understand it rather than memorizing a definition.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nHashMap stores entries in an array of buckets. For each key, it computes hashCode(), applies an internal spreading/mixing function to reduce clustering, then maps the result to a bucket index using (n-1) & hash where n is the current array capacity (always a power of two). Each bucket holds entries whose keys hashed to that index, structured as a linked list (or a red-black tree if the bucket becomes large, 8+ entries with table size >= 64). Lookups (get) compute the same hash, jump to the bucket, then linearly compare keys with equals() to find the exact entry. The map automatically \"resizes\" (doubles its capacity and rehashes all entries) once the number of entries exceeds capacity * loadFactor (default load factor 0.75) to keep operations close to O(1) average time.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nMap<String, Integer> ages = new HashMap<>();\nages.put(\"Tom\", 25);\n// hash(\"Tom\") determines bucket index, entry stored there ages.put(\"Jerry\", 30);\n// hash(\"Jerry\") likely lands in a different bucket int age = ages.get(\"Tom\");\n// recompute hash(\"Tom\"), jump to same bucket, equals() confirms match -> 25\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nHashMap stores entries in an array of buckets. For each key, it computes hashCode(), applies an internal spreading/mixing function to reduce clustering, then maps the result to a bucket index using (n-1) & hash where n is the current array capacity (always a power of two). Each bucket holds entries whose keys hashed to that index, structured as a linked list (or a red-black tree if the bucket becomes large, 8+ entries with table size >= 64). Lookups (get) compute the same hash, jump to the bucket, then linearly compare keys with equals() to find the exact entry. The map automatically \"resizes\" (doubles its capacity and rehashes all entries) once the number of entries exceeds capacity * loadFactor (default load factor 0.75) to keep operations close to O(1) average time.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nMap<String, Integer> ages = new HashMap<>();\nages.put(\"Tom\", 25);\n// hash(\"Tom\") determines bucket index, entry stored there ages.put(\"Jerry\", 30);\n// hash(\"Jerry\") likely lands in a different bucket int age = ages.get(\"Tom\");\n// recompute hash(\"Tom\"), jump to same bucket, equals() confirms match -> 25\n```",
