    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nComparable is implemented by the class whose instances you want to compare \u2014 it defines a single natural ordering via the compareTo() method, and a class can only implement Comparable once (one natural order). Comparator is a separate object that defines an ordering externally, via the compare() method, allowing you to define multiple different sorting strategies for the same class without modifying the class itself (useful when you don't own the class, or need several different sort orders for different contexts, like sorting Employees by name in one place and by salary in another).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nclass Employee implements Comparable<Employee> {\n    String name;\n    double salary;\n    public int compareTo(Employee other) {\n        return this.name.compareTo(other.name);\n    }\n    // natural order: by name\n}\nList<Employee> employees = ...;\nCollections.sort(employees);\n// uses compareTo() - sorted by name\n// Comparator - alternate/external ordering, by salary\nComparator<Employee> bySalary = (e1, e2) -> Double.compare(e1.salary, e2.salary);\nemployees.sort(bySalary);\n// sorted by salary instead, without touching the Employee\nclass\n```",
