    shortDescription: "A focused Core Java & Modern Java interview question comparing map() vs filter() vs flatmap() in streams, including the key differences, when each option fits, and the trade-offs worth discussing in a Java interview.",
    explanation: "This question evaluates whether you can distinguish the alternatives in map() vs filter() vs flatmap() in streams instead of listing definitions. A strong answer should compare their behavior, explain when each is appropriate, mention important limitations, and use a small Java example or concrete scenario where it clarifies the difference.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nmap() transforms each element in a stream into another value (one-to-one transformation) and returns a new stream of the transformed elements. filter() selects elements that match a given predicate (boolean-returning function), removing elements that don't match, without transforming the remaining ones. flatMap() is used when each element itself produces a stream (or collection) of elements \u2014 it \"flattens\" a stream of streams into a single, flat stream, combining transformation and flattening in one step (useful for nested collections, like a List<List<String>> that you want to process as one flat List<String>).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nList<String> words = List.of(\"hello\", \"world\");\n// map - one-to-one transformation\nList<Integer> lengths = words.stream().map(String::length).collect(Collectors.toList());\n// [5, 5]\n// filter - selection without transformation\nList<String> longWords = words.stream().filter(w -> w.length() > 4).collect(Collectors.toList());\n// [hello, world]\n// flatMap - flattening nested structures\nList<List<Character>> nested = List.of(List.of('a','b'), List.of('c','d'));\nList<Character> flat = nested.stream().flatMap(List::stream).collect(Collectors.toList());\n// [a, b, c, d]\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nmap() transforms each element in a stream into another value (one-to-one transformation) and returns a new stream of the transformed elements. filter() selects elements that match a given predicate (boolean-returning function), removing elements that don't match, without transforming the remaining ones. flatMap() is used when each element itself produces a stream (or collection) of elements \u2014 it \"flattens\" a stream of streams into a single, flat stream, combining transformation and flattening in one step (useful for nested collections, like a List<List<String>> that you want to process as one flat List<String>).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nList<String> words = List.of(\"hello\", \"world\");\n// map - one-to-one transformation\nList<Integer> lengths = words.stream().map(String::length).collect(Collectors.toList());\n// [5, 5]\n// filter - selection without transformation\nList<String> longWords = words.stream().filter(w -> w.length() > 4).collect(Collectors.toList());\n// [hello, world]\n// flatMap - flattening nested structures\nList<List<Character>> nested = List.of(List.of('a','b'), List.of('c','d'));\nList<Character> flat = nested.stream().flatMap(List::stream).collect(Collectors.toList());\n// [a, b, c, d]\n```",
