    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nOptional<T> is a container object introduced in Java 8 that may or may not hold a non-null value, designed to make the possibility of \"absence of a value\" explicit in a method's return type, reducing accidental NullPointerExceptions and forcing callers to consciously handle the absent case (via isPresent(), orElse(), map(), ifPresent(), etc.) rather than silently receiving a null. However, Optional should NOT be used as a field type in a class (it's not Serializable and adds unnecessary wrapping overhead for internal state), should NOT be used as a method parameter type (it complicates the API \u2014 better to overload methods or use a default value instead), and should not be used for collections (an empty List is preferable to Optional<List<T>>). Its intended, idiomatic use is specifically as a return type for methods that might not have a result to give back.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Good use - as a\nreturn type public Optional<User> findUserById(Long id) {\n    User user = database.lookup(id);\n    return Optional.ofNullable(user);\n    // caller must consciously handle the \"not found\" case\n}\nOptional<User> result = findUserById(5L);\nresult.ifPresentOrElse( user -> System.out.println(\"Found: \" + user.getName()), () -> System.out.println(\"User not found\") );\n// Bad use - as a\nclass field (avoid this) class User {\n    private Optional<String> middleName;\n    // NOT recommended - use a plain nullable String field instead\n}\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nOptional<T> is a container object introduced in Java 8 that may or may not hold a non-null value, designed to make the possibility of \"absence of a value\" explicit in a method's return type, reducing accidental NullPointerExceptions and forcing callers to consciously handle the absent case (via isPresent(), orElse(), map(), ifPresent(), etc.) rather than silently receiving a null. However, Optional should NOT be used as a field type in a class (it's not Serializable and adds unnecessary wrapping overhead for internal state), should NOT be used as a method parameter type (it complicates the API \u2014 better to overload methods or use a default value instead), and should not be used for collections (an empty List is preferable to Optional<List<T>>). Its intended, idiomatic use is specifically as a return type for methods that might not have a result to give back.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Good use - as a\nreturn type public Optional<User> findUserById(Long id) {\n    User user = database.lookup(id);\n    return Optional.ofNullable(user);\n    // caller must consciously handle the \"not found\" case\n}\nOptional<User> result = findUserById(5L);\nresult.ifPresentOrElse( user -> System.out.println(\"Found: \" + user.getName()), () -> System.out.println(\"User not found\") );\n// Bad use - as a\nclass field (avoid this) class User {\n    private Optional<String> middleName;\n    // NOT recommended - use a plain nullable String field instead\n}\n```",
