    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThese three are unrelated keywords/methods despite similar spelling. final is a modifier applied to variables (makes them constants, unreassignable after initialization), methods (prevents overriding in subclasses), or classes (prevents the class from being subclassed at all, like the String class). finally is a block used with try-catch that always executes after the try (and any catch) block completes, whether or not an exception occurred, typically used for cleanup like closing resources (though try-with-resources is now preferred for that specific purpose). finalize() is a method (from Object, now deprecated since Java 9 and removed/discouraged in modern Java) that the garbage collector was supposed to call before reclaiming an object's memory, intended for last-chance cleanup, but its unpredictable timing and performance issues make it obsolete \u2014 resource cleanup should use try-with-resources or explicit close() methods instead.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nfinal int MAX = 100;\n// final variable - cannot be reassigned\nclass Base {\n    final void show() {\n    }\n}\n// final method - cannot be overridden final\nclass Constants {\n}\n// final\nclass - cannot be extended try {\n    riskyOperation();\n}\ncatch (Exception e) {\n    handleError(e);\n}\nfinally {\n    System.out.println(\"This always runs, exception or not\");\n    //\n    finally block\n}\n```",
