    explanation: "This question evaluates your understanding of how does java garbage collection work at a high level in Java. A strong answer should define the concept precisely, explain how it behaves in practice, distinguish it from commonly confused concepts, and give a concrete example or consequence that shows you understand it rather than memorizing a definition.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nJava's garbage collector automatically reclaims memory occupied by objects that are no longer reachable from any active thread, local variable, static field, or other \"GC root\" \u2014 meaning the program can no longer possibly use them. The most common approach is generational garbage collection: the heap is divided into a Young Generation (where new objects are allocated, further split into Eden and two Survivor spaces) and an Old Generation (for long-lived objects that have survived several collections). Minor GCs happen frequently and quickly in the Young Generation (since most objects die young \u2014 the \"weak generational hypothesis\"), while Major/Full GCs on the Old Generation happen less often but take longer. Objects surviving multiple minor GCs get \"promoted\" to the Old Generation. Modern collectors like G1 use a mark-and-sweep (or mark-compact) approach within regions to identify and reclaim unreachable objects, and can run mostly concurrently with the application to minimize pause times.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nObject obj = new Object();\n// allocated in the Young Generation (Eden space) obj = null;\n// no more references to the original object - it becomes eligible\nfor garbage collection\n// On the next minor GC cycle, since nothing reaches this object anymore, its memory is reclaimed.\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nJava's garbage collector automatically reclaims memory occupied by objects that are no longer reachable from any active thread, local variable, static field, or other \"GC root\" \u2014 meaning the program can no longer possibly use them. The most common approach is generational garbage collection: the heap is divided into a Young Generation (where new objects are allocated, further split into Eden and two Survivor spaces) and an Old Generation (for long-lived objects that have survived several collections). Minor GCs happen frequently and quickly in the Young Generation (since most objects die young \u2014 the \"weak generational hypothesis\"), while Major/Full GCs on the Old Generation happen less often but take longer. Objects surviving multiple minor GCs get \"promoted\" to the Old Generation. Modern collectors like G1 use a mark-and-sweep (or mark-compact) approach within regions to identify and reclaim unreachable objects, and can run mostly concurrently with the application to minimize pause times.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nObject obj = new Object();\n// allocated in the Young Generation (Eden space) obj = null;\n// no more references to the original object - it becomes eligible\nfor garbage collection\n// On the next minor GC cycle, since nothing reaches this object anymore, its memory is reclaimed.\n```",
