    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThe Stack is a region of memory that stores method call frames, including local variables and primitive values, and it's organized as a LIFO (last-in, first-out) structure \u2014 each thread has its own stack, and memory is automatically reclaimed as soon as a method returns (its stack frame is popped). The Heap is a shared region of memory (shared across all threads) where all objects (and arrays) are allocated via \"new\", and objects live there until the garbage collector determines they're no longer reachable and reclaims them \u2014 heap memory management is far more complex and slower than stack memory, but necessary because objects often need to outlive the method that created them.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\npublic void method() {\n    int x = 5;\n    // primitive local variable - stored on the Stack\n    Person p =\n    new Person();\n    // 'p' (the reference) is on the Stack, but the actual Person OBJECT is on the Heap\n}\n// when method() returns, x and the reference p are popped off the stack;\n// the Person object on the heap becomes eligible\nfor GC only if nothing else references it\n```",
