    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA race condition occurs when the correctness of a program depends on the relative timing or interleaving of multiple threads accessing shared mutable state, and different interleavings produce different (and often incorrect) results. It typically happens with compound operations (read-check-write patterns) on shared data that aren't properly synchronized \u2014 for example, two threads both reading a shared counter's current value before either writes back the incremented result, causing one increment to be \"lost.\"\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nclass Counter {\n    private int count = 0;\n    public void increment() {\n        count++;\n    }\n    // NOT atomic: read count, add 1, write count back - 3 separate steps\n}\n// If Thread A and Thread B both call increment() at nearly the same time:\n// Both might read count=5 before either writes back, both write count=6,\n// resulting in count=6 instead of the correct count=7 - one increment was \"lost\" due to the race.\n```",
