    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA deadlock happens when two or more threads each hold a lock the other needs and wait indefinitely for each other, so none can proceed. To avoid deadlock: (1) always acquire multiple locks in a fixed, consistent global order across all threads; (2) use tryLock() with a timeout instead of blocking indefinitely, so a thread can back off and retry if it can't acquire all needed locks; (3) minimize the scope and number of locks held simultaneously; (4) use higher-level concurrency utilities (like java.util.concurrent classes) that are designed to avoid manual lock ordering issues; (5) use lock-free/non-blocking data structures (like Atomic classes) where possible.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```text\n// Avoiding deadlock via consistent lock ordering void transfer(Account from, Account to, double amount) {\nAccount first = from.getId() < to.getId() ? from : to;\n// always lock lower ID first\nAccount second = from.getId() < to.getId() ? to : from;\nsynchronized (first) {\n    synchronized (second) {\n        from.debit(amount);\n        to.credit(amount);\n    }\n}\n}\n// Because every thread locks accounts in the same consistent order (by ID),\n// two threads transferring in opposite directions can never deadlock on each other.\n```",
