    explanation: "This question evaluates whether you can distinguish the alternatives in executorservice vs creating threads manually instead of listing definitions. A strong answer should compare their behavior, explain when each is appropriate, mention important limitations, and use a small Java example or concrete scenario where it clarifies the difference.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nManually creating threads (new Thread(...).start()) for every task is inefficient and risky at scale \u2014 thread creation has overhead, unbounded thread creation can exhaust system resources, and there's no built-in mechanism for reusing threads, managing task queues, or collecting results. ExecutorService (from java.util.concurrent) provides a managed thread pool abstraction: you submit tasks (Runnable or Callable) to the executor, and it handles scheduling them onto a reusable pool of worker threads, queuing excess tasks when all threads are busy, and provides lifecycle management (shutdown, awaitTermination) and result-handling via Future objects. This is the recommended, production-grade way to manage concurrency in Java rather than manual thread management.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Manual - inefficient, no pooling, no result handling built-in\nfor (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {\n    new Thread(() -> doWork()).start();\n    // creates 1000 raw threads - very wasteful\n}\n// ExecutorService - managed, reusable thread pool\nExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);\n// only 10 threads handle all 1000 tasks\nfor (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {\n    executor.submit(() -> doWork());\n}\nexecutor.shutdown();\n// gracefully stop accepting\nnew tasks, finish queued ones\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nManually creating threads (new Thread(...).start()) for every task is inefficient and risky at scale \u2014 thread creation has overhead, unbounded thread creation can exhaust system resources, and there's no built-in mechanism for reusing threads, managing task queues, or collecting results. ExecutorService (from java.util.concurrent) provides a managed thread pool abstraction: you submit tasks (Runnable or Callable) to the executor, and it handles scheduling them onto a reusable pool of worker threads, queuing excess tasks when all threads are busy, and provides lifecycle management (shutdown, awaitTermination) and result-handling via Future objects. This is the recommended, production-grade way to manage concurrency in Java rather than manual thread management.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Manual - inefficient, no pooling, no result handling built-in\nfor (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {\n    new Thread(() -> doWork()).start();\n    // creates 1000 raw threads - very wasteful\n}\n// ExecutorService - managed, reusable thread pool\nExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);\n// only 10 threads handle all 1000 tasks\nfor (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {\n    executor.submit(() -> doWork());\n}\nexecutor.shutdown();\n// gracefully stop accepting\nnew tasks, finish queued ones\n```",
