    explanation: "This question evaluates whether you can distinguish the alternatives in future vs completablefuture instead of listing definitions. A strong answer should compare their behavior, explain when each is appropriate, mention important limitations, and use a small Java example or concrete scenario where it clarifies the difference.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nFuture represents the result of an asynchronous computation but has limited capabilities \u2014 you can only check if it's done (isDone()) or block and wait for the result (get()), with no way to chain further actions, combine multiple futures, or be notified/callback when it completes without blocking. CompletableFuture (Java 8+) is a much richer implementation of Future that supports composing and chaining asynchronous operations declaratively (thenApply, thenCompose, thenCombine), combining multiple futures, adding callbacks for completion/exception (whenComplete, exceptionally) without blocking, and manually completing a future from outside. It essentially brings a full functional, non-blocking asynchronous programming model to Java.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Future - can only block and wait, no chaining\nExecutorService executor = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();\nFuture<Integer> future = executor.submit(() -> computeValue());\nint result = future.get();\n// blocks the calling thread until done\n// CompletableFuture - can chain non-blocking operations\nCompletableFuture<Integer> cf = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> computeValue()) .thenApply(value -> value * 2)\n// transform the result once ready .thenApply(value -> value + 10);\n// chain another transformation cf.thenAccept(result -> System.out.println(\"Final result: \" + result));\n// callback, non-blocking\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nFuture represents the result of an asynchronous computation but has limited capabilities \u2014 you can only check if it's done (isDone()) or block and wait for the result (get()), with no way to chain further actions, combine multiple futures, or be notified/callback when it completes without blocking. CompletableFuture (Java 8+) is a much richer implementation of Future that supports composing and chaining asynchronous operations declaratively (thenApply, thenCompose, thenCombine), combining multiple futures, adding callbacks for completion/exception (whenComplete, exceptionally) without blocking, and manually completing a future from outside. It essentially brings a full functional, non-blocking asynchronous programming model to Java.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Future - can only block and wait, no chaining\nExecutorService executor = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();\nFuture<Integer> future = executor.submit(() -> computeValue());\nint result = future.get();\n// blocks the calling thread until done\n// CompletableFuture - can chain non-blocking operations\nCompletableFuture<Integer> cf = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> computeValue()) .thenApply(value -> value * 2)\n// transform the result once ready .thenApply(value -> value + 10);\n// chain another transformation cf.thenAccept(result -> System.out.println(\"Final result: \" + result));\n// callback, non-blocking\n```",
