    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nOptions include: synchronizing the entire getInstance() method (simple but slower due to lock contention on every call); double-checked locking with a volatile instance field (checks null twice, only synchronizing on the first-ever creation, avoiding lock overhead on subsequent calls); the \"Initialization-on-demand holder\" idiom (using a static nested class that the JVM guarantees is loaded lazily and thread-safely only when first accessed); or simply declaring the singleton as an enum with one constant, which the JVM inherently guarantees is instantiated exactly once, thread-safely, even handling serialization correctly.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Initialization-on-demand holder idiom - lazy, thread-safe, no explicit synchronization needed\npublic class ConfigManager {\n    private ConfigManager() {\n    }\n    private static class Holder {\n        private static final ConfigManager INSTANCE = new ConfigManager();\n        // loaded only when Holder is first accessed\n    }\n    public static ConfigManager getInstance() {\n        return Holder.INSTANCE;\n        // JVM\n        class-loading guarantees are thread-safe by specification\n    }\n}\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nOptions include: synchronizing the entire getInstance() method (simple but slower due to lock contention on every call); double-checked locking with a volatile instance field (checks null twice, only synchronizing on the first-ever creation, avoiding lock overhead on subsequent calls); the \"Initialization-on-demand holder\" idiom (using a static nested class that the JVM guarantees is loaded lazily and thread-safely only when first accessed); or simply declaring the singleton as an enum with one constant, which the JVM inherently guarantees is instantiated exactly once, thread-safely, even handling serialization correctly.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Initialization-on-demand holder idiom - lazy, thread-safe, no explicit synchronization needed\npublic class ConfigManager {\n    private ConfigManager() {\n    }\n    private static class Holder {\n        private static final ConfigManager INSTANCE = new ConfigManager();\n        // loaded only when Holder is first accessed\n    }\n    public static ConfigManager getInstance() {\n        return Holder.INSTANCE;\n        // JVM\n        class-loading guarantees are thread-safe by specification\n    }\n}\n```",
