    explanation: "This question evaluates your understanding of what is the jit compiler in Java. A strong answer should define the concept precisely, explain how it behaves in practice, distinguish it from commonly confused concepts, and give a concrete example or consequence that shows you understand it rather than memorizing a definition.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThe Just-In-Time (JIT) compiler is a component of the JVM that improves runtime performance by compiling frequently executed bytecode (\"hot\" methods/loops) into optimized native machine code on the fly, rather than continuing to interpret bytecode line-by-line every single time. The JVM initially runs bytecode through an interpreter for quick startup, but profiles execution to detect \"hot spots\" (hence \"HotSpot\" JVM), and then invokes the JIT compiler to compile those hot methods into native code, applying advanced optimizations like inlining, loop unrolling, and dead code elimination that aren't possible with pure interpretation. This gives Java the fast startup of an interpreted language combined with near-native performance for long-running, CPU-intensive code paths.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```text\n// A method called millions of times in a loop\nfor (int i = 0; i < 10_000_000; i++) {\n    result += compute(i);\n    // after the JVM detects this is a \"hot\" method,\n    // the JIT compiles compute() into optimized native machine code,\n    // making later iterations run much faster than the initial interpreted execution }\n```",
