    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThe investigation approach: (1) Capture a heap dump at (or just before) the crash, either automatically via the JVM flag -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError -XX:HeapDumpPath=/path, or manually using jmap; (2) Analyze the heap dump with a tool like Eclipse MAT (Memory Analyzer Tool) or VisualVM, looking at the \"dominator tree\" or \"leak suspects report\" to identify which objects are consuming the most retained memory and what's holding references to them (preventing garbage collection); (3) Check for common leak patterns: static collections that grow unbounded, unclosed resources (listeners, streams, connections not released), caches without eviction policies, or ThreadLocal variables not cleaned up in thread-pooled environments; (4) Review GC logs (enabled via -Xlog:gc) to see if the heap is genuinely growing over time (a leak) versus just needing a larger heap size (-Xmx) for legitimate memory needs; (5) Reproduce under load in a controlled environment with monitoring enabled to correlate the growth with specific application actions.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```text\n# JVM flags to auto-capture a heap dump on OOM\njava -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError -XX:HeapDumpPath=/var/dumps/app.hprof -jar app.jar\n// After loading app.hprof into Eclipse MAT, you might discover:\n// \"com.example.CacheManager\" holds a HashMap with 2 million entries that's never evicted,\n// consuming 1.2 GB of retained heap - the root cause of the OutOfMemoryError.\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThe investigation approach: (1) Capture a heap dump at (or just before) the crash, either automatically via the JVM flag -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError -XX:HeapDumpPath=/path, or manually using jmap; (2) Analyze the heap dump with a tool like Eclipse MAT (Memory Analyzer Tool) or VisualVM, looking at the \"dominator tree\" or \"leak suspects report\" to identify which objects are consuming the most retained memory and what's holding references to them (preventing garbage collection); (3) Check for common leak patterns: static collections that grow unbounded, unclosed resources (listeners, streams, connections not released), caches without eviction policies, or ThreadLocal variables not cleaned up in thread-pooled environments; (4) Review GC logs (enabled via -Xlog:gc) to see if the heap is genuinely growing over time (a leak) versus just needing a larger heap size (-Xmx) for legitimate memory needs; (5) Reproduce under load in a controlled environment with monitoring enabled to correlate the growth with specific application actions.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```text\n# JVM flags to auto-capture a heap dump on OOM\njava -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError -XX:HeapDumpPath=/var/dumps/app.hprof -jar app.jar\n// After loading app.hprof into Eclipse MAT, you might discover:\n// \"com.example.CacheManager\" holds a HashMap with 2 million entries that's never evicted,\n// consuming 1.2 GB of retained heap - the root cause of the OutOfMemoryError.\n```",
