    explanation: "This question evaluates your understanding of what is jdbc and what are the normal steps to execute a query in Java. A strong answer should define the concept precisely, explain how it behaves in practice, distinguish it from commonly confused concepts, and give a concrete example or consequence that shows you understand it rather than memorizing a definition.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nJDBC (Java Database Connectivity) is a standard Java API that provides a vendor-neutral way to connect to and interact with relational databases using SQL. The normal steps are: (1) Load the database driver (mostly automatic in modern JDBC via ServiceLoader, previously required Class.forName()); (2) Establish a Connection using DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, password); (3) Create a Statement or PreparedStatement from the connection; (4) Execute the query (executeQuery() for SELECT, executeUpdate() for INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE); (5) Process the ResultSet if applicable, iterating with next() and extracting column values; (6) Close all resources (ResultSet, Statement, Connection) in reverse order, ideally using try-with-resources to guarantee cleanup even on exceptions.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\nString sql = \"SELECT name, salary\n  FROM employees\n  WHERE id = ?\";\n  try (Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, password); PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {\n  ps.setInt(1, 101);\n  try (ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {\n  while (rs.next()) {\n  System.out.println(rs.getString(\"name\") + \": \" + rs.getDouble(\"salary\"));\n  }\n  }\n  }\n  // all resources auto-closed here\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nJDBC (Java Database Connectivity) is a standard Java API that provides a vendor-neutral way to connect to and interact with relational databases using SQL. The normal steps are: (1) Load the database driver (mostly automatic in modern JDBC via ServiceLoader, previously required Class.forName()); (2) Establish a Connection using DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, password); (3) Create a Statement or PreparedStatement from the connection; (4) Execute the query (executeQuery() for SELECT, executeUpdate() for INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE); (5) Process the ResultSet if applicable, iterating with next() and extracting column values; (6) Close all resources (ResultSet, Statement, Connection) in reverse order, ideally using try-with-resources to guarantee cleanup even on exceptions.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\nString sql = \"SELECT name, salary\n  FROM employees\n  WHERE id = ?\";\n  try (Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, password); PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {\n  ps.setInt(1, 101);\n  try (ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {\n  while (rs.next()) {\n  System.out.println(rs.getString(\"name\") + \": \" + rs.getDouble(\"salary\"));\n  }\n  }\n  }\n  // all resources auto-closed here\n```",
