    explanation: "This question evaluates whether you can distinguish the alternatives in statement vs preparedstatement instead of listing definitions. A strong answer should compare their behavior, explain when each is appropriate, mention important limitations, and use a small Java example or concrete scenario where it clarifies the difference.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nStatement executes a static SQL string as-is every time \u2014 it must be re-parsed and re-compiled by the database on every execution, and it's vulnerable to SQL injection if you build queries by concatenating user input directly into the SQL string. PreparedStatement is precompiled with placeholders (?) for parameters, which the database can cache and reuse the execution plan for across multiple calls (better performance for repeated queries), and crucially, it safely binds parameter values separately from the SQL structure, so user input can never be interpreted as SQL code \u2014 eliminating SQL injection risk for those parameters.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\n// Statement - vulnerable to SQL injection, string concatenation\n  String userInput = \"101 OR 1=1\";\n  // malicious input\n  Statement stmt = conn.createStatement();\n  ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery(\"SELECT *\n  FROM employees\n  WHERE id = \" + userInput);\n  // DANGEROUS\n  // PreparedStatement - safe, parameterized\n  PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(\"SELECT *\n  FROM employees\n  WHERE id = ?\");\n  ps.setString(1, userInput);\n  // treated strictly as a literal value, not executable SQL - safe\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nStatement executes a static SQL string as-is every time \u2014 it must be re-parsed and re-compiled by the database on every execution, and it's vulnerable to SQL injection if you build queries by concatenating user input directly into the SQL string. PreparedStatement is precompiled with placeholders (?) for parameters, which the database can cache and reuse the execution plan for across multiple calls (better performance for repeated queries), and crucially, it safely binds parameter values separately from the SQL structure, so user input can never be interpreted as SQL code \u2014 eliminating SQL injection risk for those parameters.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\n// Statement - vulnerable to SQL injection, string concatenation\n  String userInput = \"101 OR 1=1\";\n  // malicious input\n  Statement stmt = conn.createStatement();\n  ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery(\"SELECT *\n  FROM employees\n  WHERE id = \" + userInput);\n  // DANGEROUS\n  // PreparedStatement - safe, parameterized\n  PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(\"SELECT *\n  FROM employees\n  WHERE id = ?\");\n  ps.setString(1, userInput);\n  // treated strictly as a literal value, not executable SQL - safe\n```",
