    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA Servlet's lifecycle is managed by the Servlet container (like Tomcat) and consists of three main phases: (1) init() \u2014 called exactly once when the servlet is first loaded into memory (either at container startup or on first request), used for one-time setup like initializing resources; (2) service() \u2014 called for every incoming request, and internally it dispatches to doGet(), doPost(), doPut(), doDelete(), etc. based on the HTTP method, handling the actual request/response logic; (3) destroy() \u2014 called exactly once when the servlet is being taken out of service (container shutdown or servlet unloading), used for cleanup like releasing resources. A single servlet instance is typically reused across many requests/threads, which is why servlets should generally be stateless or carefully synchronized if they hold instance state.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\npublic class MyServlet extends HttpServlet {\n    @Override public void init() {\n        System.out.println(\"Servlet initialized - runs once\");\n    }\n    @Override protected void doGet(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse resp) {\n        // runs on every\n        GET request, potentially from multiple threads concurrently\n    }\n    @Override public void destroy() {\n        System.out.println(\"Servlet destroyed - runs once at shutdown\");\n    }\n}\n```",
