    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nSince HTTP is inherently stateless (each request is independent, with no memory of previous requests), sessions provide a way to maintain state across multiple requests from the same client. When a client first interacts with a server, the server creates a session object (identified by a unique Session ID) and sends this ID back to the client, typically stored as a cookie (JSESSIONID in Java web apps) or, if cookies are disabled, appended to URLs (URL rewriting). On subsequent requests, the client automatically sends this session ID back, and the server uses it to look up and retrieve the associated session data (stored server-side, e.g., in memory, a database, or a distributed cache like Redis for scalability across multiple server instances). Sessions typically expire after a period of inactivity (timeout) or can be explicitly invalidated (e.g., on logout).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Server-side, storing data in a session (Java Servlet API)\nHttpSession session = request.getSession();\n// creates one\nif it doesn't exist session.setAttribute(\"username\", \"alice\");\n// On a later request, retrieving it\n HttpSession session = request.getSession(false);\n// false = don't create a\nnew one if absent String username = (String) session.getAttribute(\"username\");\n// \"alice\"\n// Behind the scenes, the browser sent a \"Cookie: JSESSIONID=ABC123...\" header\n// that the server used to find this exact session object.\n```",
