    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nConstructor injection is preferred because: (1) it makes dependencies explicit and mandatory \u2014 you cannot create the object without supplying all required dependencies, preventing incomplete/invalid object states; (2) it allows fields to be declared \"final\", enforcing immutability and thread-safety; (3) it makes unit testing straightforward \u2014 you can instantiate the class directly with mock dependencies via the constructor, without needing a Spring container or reflection; (4) it clearly surfaces a class's dependencies in one place (the constructor signature), making the code easier to read and reason about \u2014 if a class needs too many constructor parameters, it's an early warning sign the class may be violating the single responsibility principle; (5) Spring's own documentation explicitly recommends it as the default approach, reserving setter injection for genuinely optional dependencies.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@Service public class OrderService {\n    private final PaymentGateway paymentGateway;\n    // final - guaranteed set once, immutable\n    private final InventoryService inventoryService;\n    public OrderService(PaymentGateway paymentGateway, InventoryService inventoryService) {\n        this.paymentGateway = paymentGateway;\n        this.inventoryService = inventoryService;\n    }\n}\n// Easy to unit test without Spring:\nOrderService service =\nnew OrderService(new MockPaymentGateway(), new MockInventoryService());\n```",
