    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\n@Value is used to inject a single configuration property (from application.properties/yml, environment variables, or system properties) into a single field, using SpEL-like syntax (${property.name}) \u2014 it's simple but becomes cumbersome and error-prone when you have many related properties to inject (repetitive, no type-safety across a group, no built-in validation). @ConfigurationProperties binds an entire group of related, hierarchical properties (with a common prefix) into a single strongly-typed Java class (a POJO) all at once, supporting nested objects, lists, and maps, and integrates well with Bean Validation (@Validated) for validating configuration at startup \u2014 making it the better choice for anything beyond a couple of standalone values.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n# application.properties app.mail.host=smtp.example.com app.mail.port=587 app.mail.from-address=noreply\n@example.com\n//\n@Value - injecting one property at a time (repetitive for multiple related properties)\n@Value(\"${app.mail.host}\") private String mailHost;\n//\n@ConfigurationProperties - binds the whole group into one typed object\n@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = \"app.mail\")\n@Component public class MailProperties {\n    private String host;\n    private int port;\n    private String fromAddress;\n    // getters and setters - Spring binds all three properties automatically\n}\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\n@Value is used to inject a single configuration property (from application.properties/yml, environment variables, or system properties) into a single field, using SpEL-like syntax (${property.name}) \u2014 it's simple but becomes cumbersome and error-prone when you have many related properties to inject (repetitive, no type-safety across a group, no built-in validation). @ConfigurationProperties binds an entire group of related, hierarchical properties (with a common prefix) into a single strongly-typed Java class (a POJO) all at once, supporting nested objects, lists, and maps, and integrates well with Bean Validation (@Validated) for validating configuration at startup \u2014 making it the better choice for anything beyond a couple of standalone values.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n# application.properties app.mail.host=smtp.example.com app.mail.port=587 app.mail.from-address=noreply\n@example.com\n//\n@Value - injecting one property at a time (repetitive for multiple related properties)\n@Value(\"${app.mail.host}\") private String mailHost;\n//\n@ConfigurationProperties - binds the whole group into one typed object\n@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = \"app.mail\")\n@Component public class MailProperties {\n    private String host;\n    private int port;\n    private String fromAddress;\n    // getters and setters - Spring binds all three properties automatically\n}\n```",
