    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nBoth are mechanisms Spring uses under the hood to create proxy objects for AOP and @Transactional support. A JDK dynamic proxy is created using Java's built-in java.lang.reflect.Proxy and can only proxy classes that implement at least one interface \u2014 the proxy implements the same interface(s) and delegates calls to the real object, adding advice around them. A CGLIB proxy is used when the target class does NOT implement any interface \u2014 CGLIB works by generating a subclass of the target class at runtime (bytecode manipulation), overriding its methods to insert the AOP advice, which is why CGLIB proxies cannot work on \"final\" classes or \"final\" methods (which can't be overridden/subclassed). Spring Boot defaults to using CGLIB proxies for all beans (even ones with interfaces) since Spring Boot 2.0 for consistency, unless configured otherwise.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// JDK dynamic proxy - requires an\ninterface interface PaymentService {\n    void pay(double amount);\n}\nclass PaymentServiceImpl implements PaymentService {\n    public void pay(double amount) {\n        ...\n    }\n}\n// Spring can create a JDK proxy implementing PaymentService\n// CGLIB proxy - works even without an\ninterface, by subclassing class ReportGenerator {\n    public void generate() {\n        ...\n    }\n}\n// no\ninterface\n// Spring creates a CGLIB proxy that is a runtime-generated SUBCLASS of ReportGenerator\n// NOTE:\nif generate() were declared 'final', CGLIB could NOT override/proxy it - AOP advice would silently not apply\n```",
