    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nJPA (Java Persistence API) is a specification (not an implementation) that defines a standard set of interfaces and annotations for object-relational mapping in Java \u2014 how Java objects (entities) map to database tables, how relationships between entities are defined, and how queries are written (via JPQL, an object-oriented query language similar to SQL). Being a specification, JPA itself doesn't do any actual work; it needs a concrete implementation (a \"JPA provider\") to function \u2014 the most popular implementation is Hibernate, though EclipseLink and OpenJPA also exist. Coding against the JPA interfaces (rather than Hibernate-specific APIs directly) makes your persistence code more portable across different JPA providers.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// This code uses only standard JPA annotations/interfaces - not Hibernate-specific classes\n@Entity public class Product {\n    @Id\n    @GeneratedValue private Long id;\n    private String name;\n}\n// EntityManager is the standard JPA\ninterface for persistence operations (implemented internally by Hibernate) EntityManager em = entityManagerFactory.createEntityManager();\nProduct p = em.find(Product.class, 1L);\n```",
