    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nJPA is the specification/standard (a set of interfaces and rules defined by Jakarta EE, formerly Java EE) describing HOW object-relational mapping should work in Java, without providing any actual implementation. Hibernate is a concrete implementation (a \"JPA provider\") of that specification \u2014 it's the actual library that does the real work of generating SQL, managing sessions, caching, and so on. You can think of JPA as an interface/contract and Hibernate as one of several classes that implement that contract. Hibernate also offers additional features beyond the JPA specification (like its own native Criteria API, custom annotations, and specific caching strategies), so if you use Hibernate-specific features directly, your code becomes tied to Hibernate rather than being portable to other JPA providers.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Pure JPA - portable across any provider (Hibernate, EclipseLink, etc.)\n@PersistenceContext private EntityManager entityManager;\n// standard JPA\ninterface\n// Hibernate-specific - ties your code to Hibernate specifically\nSession session = entityManager.unwrap(Session.class);\n// Hibernate's native Session API session.saveOrUpdate(product);\n// a Hibernate-specific method, not part of the JPA spec\n```",
