    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nIn JPA/Hibernate, every entity object passes through defined lifecycle states: Transient \u2014 a newly created object (via \"new\") that isn't yet associated with any persistence context/session and has no corresponding database row; Persistent (Managed) \u2014 the object is associated with an active persistence context (session), and any changes to it are tracked and automatically synchronized to the database (dirty checking) when the transaction commits or the session flushes; Detached \u2014 the object was previously persistent, but its persistence context/session has since been closed, so changes to it are no longer automatically tracked (though it can be reattached via merge()); Removed \u2014 the object has been marked for deletion (via remove()/delete()), and its corresponding database row will be deleted upon transaction commit.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nProduct p = new Product(\"Laptop\", 999.99);\n// Transient - not yet known to Hibernate em.persist(p);\n// now Persistent/Managed - tracked by the persistence context p.setPrice(899.99);\n// automatically detected by dirty checking, will be saved on commit, no explicit save() call needed em.detach(p);\n// now Detached - changes no longer tracked p.setPrice(799.99);\n// this change is silently ignored by Hibernate unless you call merge()\nProduct managedAgain = em.merge(p);\n// re-attaches a copy, now Persistent again, changes will be saved\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nIn JPA/Hibernate, every entity object passes through defined lifecycle states: Transient \u2014 a newly created object (via \"new\") that isn't yet associated with any persistence context/session and has no corresponding database row; Persistent (Managed) \u2014 the object is associated with an active persistence context (session), and any changes to it are tracked and automatically synchronized to the database (dirty checking) when the transaction commits or the session flushes; Detached \u2014 the object was previously persistent, but its persistence context/session has since been closed, so changes to it are no longer automatically tracked (though it can be reattached via merge()); Removed \u2014 the object has been marked for deletion (via remove()/delete()), and its corresponding database row will be deleted upon transaction commit.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nProduct p = new Product(\"Laptop\", 999.99);\n// Transient - not yet known to Hibernate em.persist(p);\n// now Persistent/Managed - tracked by the persistence context p.setPrice(899.99);\n// automatically detected by dirty checking, will be saved on commit, no explicit save() call needed em.detach(p);\n// now Detached - changes no longer tracked p.setPrice(799.99);\n// this change is silently ignored by Hibernate unless you call merge()\nProduct managedAgain = em.merge(p);\n// re-attaches a copy, now Persistent again, changes will be saved\n```",
