    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nDiagnosis: enable SQL logging (spring.jpa.show-sql=true, or better, use a tool like p6spy or Hibernate statistics/datasource-proxy to count actual queries executed per request) and watch for a suspicious pattern of one query followed by many nearly-identical queries differing only by an ID parameter. Fixes: (1) Use JOIN FETCH in a JPQL/HQL query to eagerly load the association in the SAME query as the parent; (2) Use @EntityGraph to declaratively specify which associations to fetch eagerly for a specific query method, without changing the entity's default fetch type globally; (3) Use batch fetching (@BatchSize annotation or hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size) so that instead of N individual queries, Hibernate groups them into fewer queries using an IN clause; (4) For read-heavy/reporting scenarios, consider projecting directly to DTOs with a single custom query instead of loading full entity graphs at all.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\n// Diagnosis - enabling SQL logging reveals the repeated query pattern\n  spring.jpa.show-sql=true spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.generate_statistics=true\n  // Fix 1:\n  JOIN FETCH @Query(\"SELECT d\n  FROM Department d\n  JOIN FETCH d.employees\") List<Department> findAllWithEmployees();\n  // single query using a SQL\n  JOIN instead of N+1\n  // Fix 2:\n  @EntityGraph @EntityGraph(attributePaths = {\n  \"employees\"\n  }\n  ) List<Department> findAll();\n  // Spring Data JPA fetches employees eagerly just\n  for this method\n```",
