    explanation: "This question evaluates whether you can distinguish the alternatives in first-level vs second-level hibernate cache instead of listing definitions. A strong answer should compare their behavior, explain when each is appropriate, mention important limitations, and use a small Java example or concrete scenario where it clarifies the difference.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nFirst-level cache is enabled by default, scoped to a single Session, and caches entities by their identifier for the duration of that session \u2014 you get it for free with no configuration. Second-level cache is optional, application/SessionFactory-wide (shared across all sessions), requires an external cache provider (Ehcache, Redis, Caffeine, Infinispan), and is best used for reference/lookup data that's read often but rarely modified.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// First-level (automatic, per-session)\nEmployee e1 = session.get(Employee.class, 1L);\n// hits DB\nEmployee e2 = session.get(Employee.class, 1L);\n// same session - returns cached instance, no DB hit\n// Second-level (requires setup) - shared across ALL sessions/application-wide\n@Cacheable\n@org.hibernate.annotations.Cache(usage = CacheConcurrencyStrategy.READ_ONLY) class Country {\n    @Id Long id;\n    String name;\n}\n// e.g., a\nstatic list of countries - great L2 cache candidate\n```",
