    explanation: "This question evaluates your understanding of what are cascade types in jpa in Java. A strong answer should define the concept precisely, explain how it behaves in practice, distinguish it from commonly confused concepts, and give a concrete example or consequence that shows you understand it rather than memorizing a definition.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nCascade types define how operations performed on a parent entity should automatically \"cascade\" (propagate) to its related/associated entities, so you don't have to manually persist, update, or delete each related entity separately. The main cascade types are: PERSIST (saving the parent also saves new related entities), MERGE (updating/merging the parent also merges related entities), REMOVE (deleting the parent also deletes related entities), REFRESH (refreshing the parent also refreshes related entities from the DB), DETACH (detaching the parent also detaches related entities), and ALL (applies all of the above). Choosing cascade types carefully matters \u2014 for example, cascading REMOVE on a shared/reference entity (like cascading delete from Order down to a shared Product) could unintentionally delete data still needed elsewhere.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@Entity class Order {\n    @OneToMany(mappedBy = \"order\", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true) private List<OrderItem> items;\n    // saving/deleting an Order automatically saves/deletes its OrderItems too\n}\nOrder order = new Order();\norder.getItems().add(new OrderItem(\"Widget\", 2));\nentityManager.persist(order);\n// CascadeType.PERSIST automatically also persists the\nnew OrderItem - no separate save needed\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nCascade types define how operations performed on a parent entity should automatically \"cascade\" (propagate) to its related/associated entities, so you don't have to manually persist, update, or delete each related entity separately. The main cascade types are: PERSIST (saving the parent also saves new related entities), MERGE (updating/merging the parent also merges related entities), REMOVE (deleting the parent also deletes related entities), REFRESH (refreshing the parent also refreshes related entities from the DB), DETACH (detaching the parent also detaches related entities), and ALL (applies all of the above). Choosing cascade types carefully matters \u2014 for example, cascading REMOVE on a shared/reference entity (like cascading delete from Order down to a shared Product) could unintentionally delete data still needed elsewhere.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@Entity class Order {\n    @OneToMany(mappedBy = \"order\", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true) private List<OrderItem> items;\n    // saving/deleting an Order automatically saves/deletes its OrderItems too\n}\nOrder order = new Order();\norder.getItems().add(new OrderItem(\"Widget\", 2));\nentityManager.persist(order);\n// CascadeType.PERSIST automatically also persists the\nnew OrderItem - no separate save needed\n```",
