    explanation: "This question evaluates your understanding of what does orphanremoval do in Java. A strong answer should define the concept precisely, explain how it behaves in practice, distinguish it from commonly confused concepts, and give a concrete example or consequence that shows you understand it rather than memorizing a definition.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\norphanRemoval = true, set on a @OneToMany or @OneToOne relationship, tells JPA/Hibernate to automatically delete a child entity from the database when it's removed from its parent's collection (or the reference is set to null) \u2014 even if you never explicitly call remove()/delete() on that child directly. This is useful for true parent-child \"ownership\" relationships, where a child entity has no meaningful existence independent of its parent (like OrderItems belonging exclusively to one Order) \u2014 removing it from the parent's list should mean it's genuinely deleted, not just \"unlinked.\"\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@Entity class Order {\n    @OneToMany(mappedBy = \"order\", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true) private List<OrderItem> items;\n}\nOrder order = orderRepository.findById(1L).get();\norder.getItems().remove(0);\n// simply removing from the in-memory list...\n// ...causes Hibernate to automatically DELETE that specific OrderItem row from the database on flush/commit,\n// because orphanRemoval=true - no explicit orderItemRepository.delete(item) call needed.\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\norphanRemoval = true, set on a @OneToMany or @OneToOne relationship, tells JPA/Hibernate to automatically delete a child entity from the database when it's removed from its parent's collection (or the reference is set to null) \u2014 even if you never explicitly call remove()/delete() on that child directly. This is useful for true parent-child \"ownership\" relationships, where a child entity has no meaningful existence independent of its parent (like OrderItems belonging exclusively to one Order) \u2014 removing it from the parent's list should mean it's genuinely deleted, not just \"unlinked.\"\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@Entity class Order {\n    @OneToMany(mappedBy = \"order\", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true) private List<OrderItem> items;\n}\nOrder order = orderRepository.findById(1L).get();\norder.getItems().remove(0);\n// simply removing from the in-memory list...\n// ...causes Hibernate to automatically DELETE that specific OrderItem row from the database on flush/commit,\n// because orphanRemoval=true - no explicit orderItemRepository.delete(item) call needed.\n```",
