    explanation: "This question evaluates whether you can distinguish the alternatives in jpql vs native sql instead of listing definitions. A strong answer should compare their behavior, explain when each is appropriate, mention important limitations, and use a small Java example or concrete scenario where it clarifies the difference.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nJPQL (Java Persistence Query Language) is an object-oriented query language defined by the JPA specification \u2014 it queries against entity names and their fields (not table/column names directly), making it database-portable across different underlying databases (since JPA translates JPQL into the appropriate SQL dialect automatically) and letting you navigate entity relationships directly in the query. Native SQL is actual, raw SQL specific to your database engine, used via @Query(nativeQuery = true) or entityManager.createNativeQuery() \u2014 you'd use it when you need database-specific features JPQL doesn't support (like specific functions, complex window functions, or vendor-specific optimizer hints), at the cost of losing database portability.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\n// JPQL - queries the Employee ENTITY and its fields, portable across databases\n  @Query(\"SELECT e\n  FROM Employee e\n  WHERE e.department.name = :deptName\") List<Employee> findByDepartmentName(@Param(\"deptName\") String deptName);\n  // Native SQL - queries actual TABLE and COLUMN names, database-specific\n  @Query(value = \"SELECT *\n  FROM employees e\n  WHERE e.dept_name = :deptName\", nativeQuery = true) List<Employee> findByDepartmentNameNative(@Param(\"deptName\") String deptName);\n```",
