    explanation: "This question evaluates your understanding of how do pageable and sort work in Java. A strong answer should define the concept precisely, explain how it behaves in practice, distinguish it from commonly confused concepts, and give a concrete example or consequence that shows you understand it rather than memorizing a definition.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nPageable is a Spring Data interface representing pagination information (page number, page size, and optionally sort order) that you pass into a repository method; Spring Data JPA translates it into the appropriate LIMIT/OFFSET SQL clauses under the hood and returns a Page<T> object, which includes not just the data for that page but also metadata like total elements and total pages, useful for building paginated UIs. Sort is a related class specifying ordering criteria (which fields, ascending/descending) that can be used standalone or combined with a Pageable (via PageRequest.of(page, size, sort)).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\npublic interface ProductRepository extends JpaRepository<Product, Long> {\n    Page<Product> findByCategory(String category, Pageable pageable);\n}\n// Usage:\nPageable pageable = PageRequest.of(0, 20, Sort.by(\"price\").descending());\n// page 0, 20 items per page, sorted\nPage<Product> page = productRepository.findByCategory(\"Electronics\", pageable);\nSystem.out.println(\"Total pages: \" + page.getTotalPages());\nSystem.out.println(\"Current page items: \" + page.getContent());\n```",
