    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\n@Modifying is used alongside @Query in Spring Data JPA repository methods to indicate that the query performs an UPDATE or DELETE operation (rather than a SELECT) \u2014 without it, Spring Data JPA would attempt to interpret the query as expecting a result set to be returned, which would fail for a bulk update/delete. It's often combined with @Transactional (since modifying operations must run within a transaction) and, optionally, clearAutomatically = true to clear the persistence context afterward (since bulk updates bypass the normal entity lifecycle/dirty checking, leaving the in-memory persistence context potentially stale).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\npublic interface EmployeeRepository extends JpaRepository<Employee, Long> {\n  @Modifying @Transactional @Query(\"UPDATE Employee e\n  SET e.salary = e.salary * 1.1\n  WHERE e.department = :dept\") int giveRaise(@Param(\"dept\") String department);\n  // returns the number of rows affected }\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\n@Modifying is used alongside @Query in Spring Data JPA repository methods to indicate that the query performs an UPDATE or DELETE operation (rather than a SELECT) \u2014 without it, Spring Data JPA would attempt to interpret the query as expecting a result set to be returned, which would fail for a bulk update/delete. It's often combined with @Transactional (since modifying operations must run within a transaction) and, optionally, clearAutomatically = true to clear the persistence context afterward (since bulk updates bypass the normal entity lifecycle/dirty checking, leaving the in-memory persistence context potentially stale).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\npublic interface EmployeeRepository extends JpaRepository<Employee, Long> {\n  @Modifying @Transactional @Query(\"UPDATE Employee e\n  SET e.salary = e.salary * 1.1\n  WHERE e.department = :dept\") int giveRaise(@Param(\"dept\") String department);\n  // returns the number of rows affected }\n```",
