    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA systematic approach: (1) Check APM/tracing tools (New Relic, Datadog, Zipkin) to identify which layer (controller, service, database, external call) is consuming the most time; (2) Enable and inspect SQL logs / Hibernate statistics to spot slow or excessive queries (particularly N+1 problems); (3) Use a profiler (JFR, async-profiler) to capture a CPU/allocation profile under load and identify hot methods; (4) Check for blocking I/O calls to external services without timeouts, which can tie up threads; (5) Review thread pool and connection pool configuration/metrics (via Actuator) for exhaustion or contention; (6) Load test with tools like JMeter or Gatling to reproduce the slowdown in a controlled environment and correlate metrics; (7) Check for unnecessary serialization/deserialization overhead or overly large response payloads.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Adding timing instrumentation via an AOP aspect to quickly pinpoint slow methods\n@Around(\"execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))\") public Object timeIt(ProceedingJoinPoint pjp) throws Throwable {\n    long start = System.nanoTime();\n    Object result = pjp.proceed();\n    long durationMs = (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000;\n    if (durationMs > 500) {\n        // flag anything over 500ms log.warn(\"{\n    }\n    took {\n    }\n    ms\", pjp.getSignature(), durationMs); } return result; }\n```",
