    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "A focused Spring Boot & Spring interview question comparing unit test vs integration test in spring, including the key differences, when each option fits, and the trade-offs worth discussing in a Java interview.",
    explanation: "This question evaluates whether you can distinguish the alternatives in unit test vs integration test in spring instead of listing definitions. A strong answer should compare their behavior, explain when each is appropriate, mention important limitations, and use a small Java example or concrete scenario where it clarifies the difference.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA unit test verifies a single class or method in isolation, typically mocking out all its dependencies (using Mockito, for example), so it runs extremely fast and pinpoints exactly which unit of code has a bug \u2014 it does NOT load the Spring context. An integration test verifies that multiple components work correctly together, often loading a (full or partial) Spring application context, an embedded/test database, and exercising real interactions across layers (e.g., an actual HTTP request through the full controller-service-repository-database stack) \u2014 it's slower but catches issues that unit tests, with all their mocked boundaries, cannot (like incorrect wiring, real SQL query bugs, or serialization issues).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Unit test - no Spring context, dependencies mocked\n@Test void testCalculateDiscount() {\n    OrderService service = new OrderService(mockPricingRepo);\n    // mocked dependency when(mockPricingRepo.getDiscount(anyString())).thenReturn(0.1); assertEquals(90.0, service.calculateDiscountedPrice(100.0, \"SUMMER10\"));\n}\n// Integration test - loads real Spring context and embedded database\n@SpringBootTest\n@AutoConfigureMockMvc class OrderControllerIntegrationTest {\n    @Autowired private MockMvc mockMvc;\n    @Test void testCreateOrder() throws Exception {\n        mockMvc.perform(post(\"/api/orders\").contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON) .content(\"{\\\"product\\\":\\\"Widget\\\",\\\"qty\\\":2}\")) .andExpect(status().isCreated());\n        // exercises the full real stack\n    }\n}\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA unit test verifies a single class or method in isolation, typically mocking out all its dependencies (using Mockito, for example), so it runs extremely fast and pinpoints exactly which unit of code has a bug \u2014 it does NOT load the Spring context. An integration test verifies that multiple components work correctly together, often loading a (full or partial) Spring application context, an embedded/test database, and exercising real interactions across layers (e.g., an actual HTTP request through the full controller-service-repository-database stack) \u2014 it's slower but catches issues that unit tests, with all their mocked boundaries, cannot (like incorrect wiring, real SQL query bugs, or serialization issues).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Unit test - no Spring context, dependencies mocked\n@Test void testCalculateDiscount() {\n    OrderService service = new OrderService(mockPricingRepo);\n    // mocked dependency when(mockPricingRepo.getDiscount(anyString())).thenReturn(0.1); assertEquals(90.0, service.calculateDiscountedPrice(100.0, \"SUMMER10\"));\n}\n// Integration test - loads real Spring context and embedded database\n@SpringBootTest\n@AutoConfigureMockMvc class OrderControllerIntegrationTest {\n    @Autowired private MockMvc mockMvc;\n    @Test void testCreateOrder() throws Exception {\n        mockMvc.perform(post(\"/api/orders\").contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON) .content(\"{\\\"product\\\":\\\"Widget\\\",\\\"qty\\\":2}\")) .andExpect(status().isCreated());\n        // exercises the full real stack\n    }\n}\n```",
