    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nIdempotency means multiple identical requests produce the same result as a single request (no duplicate side effects like double-charging a customer). A common pattern: require the client to send a unique Idempotency-Key header with each logically-distinct request; on the server, before processing, check if that key has already been seen (stored in a fast store like Redis or a database table with a unique constraint on the key) \u2014 if yes, return the previously stored response instead of re-executing the operation; if no, process the request normally and store the key alongside the response for future duplicate checks (with an appropriate expiration).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@PostMapping(\"/payments\") public ResponseEntity<PaymentResponse> createPayment(\n@RequestHeader(\"Idempotency-Key\") String idempotencyKey,\n@RequestBody PaymentRequest request) {\n    Optional<PaymentResponse> existing = idempotencyStore.get(idempotencyKey);\n    if (existing.isPresent()) {\n        return ResponseEntity.ok(existing.get());\n        //\n        return the SAME response, don't re-charge the customer } PaymentResponse response = paymentService.process(request); idempotencyStore.save(idempotencyKey, response, Duration.ofHours(24)); return ResponseEntity.ok(response); }\n```",
