    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nSensitive configuration (database passwords, API keys, encryption keys) should never be hardcoded in source code or committed to version control. Best practices: (1) use a dedicated secrets manager (AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault, Azure Key Vault, GCP Secret Manager) that securely stores and rotates secrets, injecting them into the application at runtime; (2) if using environment variables, ensure they're set through a secure deployment pipeline (not stored in plaintext files that get committed); (3) encrypt sensitive values at rest and in transit; (4) use Spring Cloud Config Server with encryption support if centralizing configuration across microservices; (5) apply the principle of least privilege \u2014 limit which services/roles can access which secrets; (6) rotate credentials periodically and immediately upon suspected compromise; (7) never log sensitive values, and mask them in Actuator's /env endpoint (Spring Boot does this automatically for keys matching common sensitive patterns like \"password\", \"secret\", \"key\").\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// BAD - hardcoded secret in source code, committed to git\nString dbPassword = \"SuperSecret123\";\n// NEVER do this\n// GOOD - fetched securely at runtime from a secrets manager\n@Value(\"${db.password}\")\n// this property is populated from AWS Secrets Manager / Vault at deploy time,\nprivate String dbPassword;\n// never stored in the codebase or plaintext config files\n```",
