    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\n(See related answer under \"investigate an OutOfMemoryError\".) Monitor heap usage trends over time via GC logs or metrics (Actuator/Micrometer's jvm.memory.used) \u2014 a true memory leak shows a heap that keeps climbing even after full GCs, never returning to a stable baseline, whereas normal memory usage sawtooths up and down as garbage collection reclaims short-lived objects. Take periodic heap dumps (or compare two heap dumps taken hours apart) and use a tool like Eclipse MAT to see which object types are growing in count/retained size between the two snapshots \u2014 this \"leak suspects\" comparison quickly narrows down the exact class responsible (commonly an ever-growing static collection, an unbounded cache, or listeners/callbacks that are registered but never deregistered).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Comparing two heap dumps taken 2 hours apart in Eclipse MAT might show:\n//\n\"com.example.SessionCache$InnerMap\" grew from 10,000 to 480,000 entries,\n//\nwhile application\ntraffic stayed roughly constant -> confirms a cache without proper eviction,\n// e.g., sessions\nare added on login but never removed on logout/timeout.\n```",
