    explanation: "This question evaluates how you would handle how would you troubleshoot database connection exhaustion in a real Java application. A strong answer should make the assumptions explicit, explain the design or troubleshooting sequence, identify failure modes and trade-offs, and finish with how you would validate the result in production.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nConnection exhaustion (all pool connections in use, new requests time out waiting) is diagnosed by: (1) monitoring pool metrics (active, idle, pending/waiting threads) via Actuator/Micrometer to confirm the pool is indeed maxed out; (2) enabling HikariCP leak detection to catch code that acquires but never releases a connection; (3) checking for long-running transactions or queries that hold a connection far longer than expected (a slow query, or a transaction that makes a slow external API call while still holding a DB connection open, is a very common culprit); (4) reviewing recent traffic patterns for an unexpected surge beyond the pool's designed capacity; (5) checking whether the configured pool size is actually appropriate for the workload (either genuinely too small, or the database server itself can't handle more connections without proportionally increasing pool size elsewhere).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// A classic anti-pattern causing connection exhaustion: holding a DB connection open\n//\nwhile making a slow external HTTP call inside the same transaction\n@Transactional public void processOrder(Order order) {\n    orderRepo.save(order);\n    // holds a DB connection shippingApiClient.notifyCarrier(order);\n    // SLOW external call - the DB connection stays checked out\n    // from the pool the entire time, unnecessarily\n}\n// Fix: move the external call outside the transactional boundary, or make it asynchronous,\n// so the database connection is released back to the pool as quickly as possible.\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nConnection exhaustion (all pool connections in use, new requests time out waiting) is diagnosed by: (1) monitoring pool metrics (active, idle, pending/waiting threads) via Actuator/Micrometer to confirm the pool is indeed maxed out; (2) enabling HikariCP leak detection to catch code that acquires but never releases a connection; (3) checking for long-running transactions or queries that hold a connection far longer than expected (a slow query, or a transaction that makes a slow external API call while still holding a DB connection open, is a very common culprit); (4) reviewing recent traffic patterns for an unexpected surge beyond the pool's designed capacity; (5) checking whether the configured pool size is actually appropriate for the workload (either genuinely too small, or the database server itself can't handle more connections without proportionally increasing pool size elsewhere).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// A classic anti-pattern causing connection exhaustion: holding a DB connection open\n//\nwhile making a slow external HTTP call inside the same transaction\n@Transactional public void processOrder(Order order) {\n    orderRepo.save(order);\n    // holds a DB connection shippingApiClient.notifyCarrier(order);\n    // SLOW external call - the DB connection stays checked out\n    // from the pool the entire time, unnecessarily\n}\n// Fix: move the external call outside the transactional boundary, or make it asynchronous,\n// so the database connection is released back to the pool as quickly as possible.\n```",
