    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nApproaches include fixed-window counters, sliding-window counters/logs, and token-bucket algorithms, typically backed by a fast, shared store like Redis so rate limits are enforced consistently across multiple application instances in a distributed deployment. Libraries like Bucket4j or Resilience4j's RateLimiter can implement this without hand-rolling the algorithm, and API gateways (like Kong, AWS API Gateway, or Spring Cloud Gateway) can also enforce rate limiting at the infrastructure layer, before requests even reach your application code.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```text\n// Using Bucket4j\nfor a token-bucket rate limiter Bandwidth limit = Bandwidth.classic(100, Refill.greedy(100, Duration.ofMinutes(1)));\n// 100 requests/minute\nBucket bucket = Bucket.builder().addLimit(limit).build();\nif (bucket.tryConsume(1)) {\n    // process the request }\n    else {\n        //\n        return HTTP 429 Too Many Requests\n    }\n```",
