    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nOptimistic locking assumes conflicts between concurrent updates are rare, so instead of locking a row for the duration of a transaction (pessimistic locking), it allows multiple transactions to read and attempt to update the same row concurrently, but detects conflicts at commit/update time using a version number (or timestamp) column. In JPA/Hibernate, you annotate a field with @Version \u2014 every UPDATE statement includes a WHERE clause checking that the version column still matches the value that was originally read; if another transaction already updated the row (incrementing the version) in the meantime, the UPDATE affects zero rows, and Hibernate throws an OptimisticLockException, signaling the caller to retry or handle the conflict.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@Entity class Product {\n    @Id private Long id;\n    private int stockQuantity;\n    @Version private Long version;\n    // Hibernate auto-manages this\n}\n// Generated UPDATE looks like:\n// UPDATE product\nSET stock_quantity = ?, version = version + 1\nWHERE id = ? AND version = ?\n// If another thread already changed 'version' in between, this UPDATE affects 0 rows,\n// and Hibernate throws an OptimisticLockException, letting the application retry the operation.\n```",
