    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA \"lost update\" happens when two concurrent transactions each read the same data, then both write back their own modified version, and one overwrites the other's changes without either transaction knowing about the conflict. This is prevented using either optimistic locking (a version column, as described above, detects and rejects the second conflicting write, forcing a retry) or pessimistic locking (explicitly locking the row for the duration of the transaction using SELECT ... FOR UPDATE, so a second transaction trying to read/modify the same row must wait until the first transaction commits or rolls back). Optimistic locking is preferred for low-contention scenarios (better throughput, no blocking), while pessimistic locking suits high-contention scenarios where conflicts are frequent and retries would be wasteful.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\n-- Pessimistic locking - the row is locked for the duration of this transaction BEGIN TRANSACTION;\n\nSELECT *\n  FROM inventory\n  WHERE product_id = 5 FOR UPDATE;\n\n-- other transactions trying to\n\nupdate this row must wait UPDATE inventory\n  SET stock = stock - 1\n  WHERE product_id = 5;\n  COMMIT;\n\n-- lock released here\n  // Optimistic locking (JPA) - see\n  @Version example above;\n  conflicts are detected, not prevented upfront\n```",
