    question: "How would you design a resilient service calling a slow external API?",
    slug: "how-would-you-design-a-resilient-service-calling-a-slow-external-api-162",
    categoryName: "Java",
    categorySlug: "java",
    subcategoryName: "REST APIs & Backend",
    subcategorySlug: "rest-apis-backend",
    experienceLevel: ExperienceLevel.EXPERIENCED,
    difficulty: Difficulty.HARD,
    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "A scenario-based REST APIs & Backend interview question about how would you design a resilient service calling a slow external api, focusing on the decisions, trade-offs, failure cases, and practical steps a strong Java candidate should explain.",
    explanation: "This question evaluates how you would handle how would you design a resilient service calling a slow external api in a real Java application. A strong answer should make the assumptions explicit, explain the design or troubleshooting sequence, identify failure modes and trade-offs, and finish with how you would validate the result in production.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nKey resilience patterns: (1) Timeouts \u2014 always set an explicit connection and read timeout on external calls, never rely on defaults (which can be infinite), to avoid threads hanging forever; (2) Retries with exponential backoff \u2014 for transient failures, retry a limited number of times with increasing delay between attempts (and ideally jitter, to avoid synchronized retry storms across many clients); (3) Circuit Breaker \u2014 using a library like Resilience4j, if calls to the external service keep failing, \"open\" the circuit to fail fast immediately (without even attempting the call) for a cooldown period, giving the struggling downstream service time to recover, then periodically test with a few \"half-open\" trial calls before fully closing the circuit again; (4) Bulkheads \u2014 isolate resources (thread pools) dedicated to a specific external dependency, so a slow/failing dependency can't exhaust shared resources needed by unrelated parts of the application; (5) Fallbacks \u2014 provide a sensible default/cached response when the external call ultimately fails, if a degraded experience is acceptable, rather than failing the entire user-facing request.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Using Resilience4j to combine timeout, retry, and circuit breaker\nCircuitBreakerConfig cbConfig = CircuitBreakerConfig.custom() .failureRateThreshold(50)\n// open the circuit\nif 50% of recent calls fail .waitDurationInOpenState(Duration.ofSeconds(30)) .build();\nCircuitBreaker circuitBreaker = CircuitBreaker.of(\"externalApi\", cbConfig);\nSupplier<String> decoratedSupplier = CircuitBreaker.decorateSupplier(circuitBreaker, () -> externalApiClient.fetchData());\nString result = Try.ofSupplier(decoratedSupplier) .recover(throwable -> \"fallback default value\")\n// graceful fallback\nif circuit is open or call fails .get();\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nKey resilience patterns: (1) Timeouts \u2014 always set an explicit connection and read timeout on external calls, never rely on defaults (which can be infinite), to avoid threads hanging forever; (2) Retries with exponential backoff \u2014 for transient failures, retry a limited number of times with increasing delay between attempts (and ideally jitter, to avoid synchronized retry storms across many clients); (3) Circuit Breaker \u2014 using a library like Resilience4j, if calls to the external service keep failing, \"open\" the circuit to fail fast immediately (without even attempting the call) for a cooldown period, giving the struggling downstream service time to recover, then periodically test with a few \"half-open\" trial calls before fully closing the circuit again; (4) Bulkheads \u2014 isolate resources (thread pools) dedicated to a specific external dependency, so a slow/failing dependency can't exhaust shared resources needed by unrelated parts of the application; (5) Fallbacks \u2014 provide a sensible default/cached response when the external call ultimately fails, if a degraded experience is acceptable, rather than failing the entire user-facing request.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Using Resilience4j to combine timeout, retry, and circuit breaker\nCircuitBreakerConfig cbConfig = CircuitBreakerConfig.custom() .failureRateThreshold(50)\n// open the circuit\nif 50% of recent calls fail .waitDurationInOpenState(Duration.ofSeconds(30)) .build();\nCircuitBreaker circuitBreaker = CircuitBreaker.of(\"externalApi\", cbConfig);\nSupplier<String> decoratedSupplier = CircuitBreaker.decorateSupplier(circuitBreaker, () -> externalApiClient.fetchData());\nString result = Try.ofSupplier(decoratedSupplier) .recover(throwable -> \"fallback default value\")\n// graceful fallback\nif circuit is open or call fails .get();\n```",
