    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nSpring provides a caching abstraction (@EnableCaching, @Cacheable, @CachePut, @CacheEvict) that works with a pluggable cache provider (an in-memory ConcurrentMapCache for simple cases, or more robust options like Caffeine for local in-JVM caching, and Redis for a distributed cache shared across multiple application instances). The key design decisions: what to cache (read-heavy, rarely-changing, or expensive-to-compute data is the best candidate), cache invalidation strategy (TTL-based expiration, explicit eviction on updates via @CacheEvict, or event-driven invalidation), and cache topology (local/in-memory caches are fastest but not shared across instances and can serve stale data after a scale-out; distributed caches like Redis are consistent across instances but add network latency and infrastructure complexity).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@Service public class ProductService {\n    @Cacheable(value = \"products\", key = \"#id\")\n    // caches the result keyed by 'id'\n    public Product getProduct(Long id) {\n        return productRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow();\n        // only actually runs on a cache miss\n    }\n    @CacheEvict(value = \"products\", key = \"#product.id\") public void updateProduct(Product product) {\n        productRepository.save(product);\n        // ensures the stale cached entry is removed after an update\n    }\n}\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nSpring provides a caching abstraction (@EnableCaching, @Cacheable, @CachePut, @CacheEvict) that works with a pluggable cache provider (an in-memory ConcurrentMapCache for simple cases, or more robust options like Caffeine for local in-JVM caching, and Redis for a distributed cache shared across multiple application instances). The key design decisions: what to cache (read-heavy, rarely-changing, or expensive-to-compute data is the best candidate), cache invalidation strategy (TTL-based expiration, explicit eviction on updates via @CacheEvict, or event-driven invalidation), and cache topology (local/in-memory caches are fastest but not shared across instances and can serve stale data after a scale-out; distributed caches like Redis are consistent across instances but add network latency and infrastructure complexity).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@Service public class ProductService {\n    @Cacheable(value = \"products\", key = \"#id\")\n    // caches the result keyed by 'id'\n    public Product getProduct(Long id) {\n        return productRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow();\n        // only actually runs on a cache miss\n    }\n    @CacheEvict(value = \"products\", key = \"#product.id\") public void updateProduct(Product product) {\n        productRepository.save(product);\n        // ensures the stale cached entry is removed after an update\n    }\n}\n```",
