    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA Redis (or similar dedicated in-memory) cache stores data outside the primary database in a very fast, in-memory key-value store, dramatically reducing latency for frequently accessed data and offloading read load from the primary database \u2014 but it introduces additional infrastructure to manage, potential cache staleness/consistency challenges (the cache and database can diverge if invalidation isn't handled carefully), and an additional network hop compared to purely in-process caching. A \"database cache\" (like a database's own query cache, buffer pool, or materialized views) keeps caching logic inside the database engine itself, requiring less additional infrastructure and often providing automatic consistency guarantees, but it's still bound by database server capacity and doesn't offload load away from the database the way an external cache does, and native query caches in many modern databases (like MySQL's now-removed query cache) have proven less effective in practice than dedicated external caching layers. In most modern high-traffic architectures, a dedicated distributed cache like Redis, placed in front of the database, is the preferred pattern for read-heavy workloads needing very low latency at scale.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Application-level caching pattern with Redis in front of the database\npublic Product getProduct(Long id) {\n    String cacheKey = \"product:\" + id;\n    String cached = redisTemplate.opsForValue().get(cacheKey);\n    if (cached != null) {\n        return deserialize(cached);\n        // fast path - avoids hitting the database entirely\n    }\n    Product product = productRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow();\n    // slow path - database read redisTemplate.opsForValue().set(cacheKey, serialize(product), Duration.ofMinutes(10));\n    // populate cache with TTL\n    return product;\n}\n```",
