    explanation: "This question evaluates how you would handle how would you safely change a large database schema with a zero-downtime deployment in a real Java application. A strong answer should make the assumptions explicit, explain the design or troubleshooting sequence, identify failure modes and trade-offs, and finish with how you would validate the result in production.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nZero-downtime schema changes typically follow an \"expand and contract\" (or \"parallel change\") pattern across multiple deployment phases, so the old and new application code can both work correctly during the transition: Phase 1 (Expand) \u2014 add the new column/table/index without removing or renaming anything old, and deploy application code that can read from both old and new structures if needed, writing to both; Phase 2 (Migrate/Backfill) \u2014 run a background job to backfill the new column/structure with data derived from the old one, without locking the whole table (batched updates, not one giant transaction); Phase 3 (Switch) \u2014 deploy application code that fully switches to using only the new structure for both reads and writes; Phase 4 (Contract) \u2014 once you're confident the new structure works correctly and the old one is unused, remove the old column/table/index in a final cleanup migration. Throughout this, use online schema change tools (like gh-ost or pt-online-schema-change for MySQL, or native online DDL support in PostgreSQL) that avoid taking long table-locking operations, and always make each migration step backward-compatible with the currently-running version of the application to support rolling deployments.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\n-- Phase 1: Expand - add new column, nullable so old code doesn't break\n\nALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN email_normalized VARCHAR(255) NULL;\n\n-- Phase 2: Backfill in batches (not one giant transaction on a huge table)\n\nUPDATE users\n  SET email_normalized = LOWER(email)\n  WHERE id BETWEEN 1 AND 10000 AND email_normalized IS NULL;\n\n-- (repeated in batches across the whole table, application still uses the OLD \"email\" column meanwhile)\n\n-- Phase 3: deploy new app version that reads/writes email_normalized instead\n\n-- Phase 4: Contract - once confirmed safe, drop the old column\n\nALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN email;\n```",
